<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>INTRODUCTION</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) are powerful AI models for understanding and generating human-like text. Prominent examples include BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, OPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite>, the GPT series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, Llama <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>, and Llama 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>. These models are built upon the Transformer architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite>, which utilizes the <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">softmax attention computation</em> as a core mechanism. Using this design, LLMs can handle a wide range of language tasks, like language modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, sentiment analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>, creative writing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, and natural language translation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The exact softmax attention computation enables models to emphasize the most influential parts of the input when generating outputs, rather than weighting all words uniformly. The attention weights, computed via a softmax function over the inputs, determine the relative importance assigned to each word. By selectively focusing on highly relevant inputs, attention layers allow models to handle longer sequences more effectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, a crucial drawback of softmax attention is its quadratic time complexity in the input length <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Given query, key, and value matrices <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K,V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K,V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and entrywise exponential function <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>exp</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\ll n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\ll n</annotation></semantics></math> is the hidden dimension, computing the <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(Q,K\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(Q,K\right)</annotation></semantics></math>-softmax attention matrix <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> and the subsequent product <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AV</annotation></semantics></math> are both computationally expensive, requiring <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{2}d\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{2}d\right)</annotation></semantics></math> time. This quadratic complexity makes it challenging for LLMs to process long input sequences efficiently. Since <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> entries, it is impossible to improve the asymptotic time complexity when explicitly computing all entries of <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, prior works on attention optimization that aim to improve quadratic complexity attempt to implicitly utilize <math id="S1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> to approximate it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Among these, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>
is the only work that provides a time-optimal algorithm—where the upper bound matches the lower bound—for approximating softmax attention without reducing it to a simpler problem. Nevertheless, their algorithm relies on strong assumptions, which makes it very difficult to implement in modern transformers.
To address this issue, we design an efficient algorithm for approximating attention via a novel mathematical technique called support-basis decomposition, eliminating the strong assumptions in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. In addition, we combine the polynomial method with the sketching to theoretically justify the competitive performance of polynomial attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, where the entrywise exponential is replaced with the entrywise polynomial <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)=x^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)=x^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Organization</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Below, we formally present the origin of the quadratic time complexity (Section <a href="#S1.SS1" title="1.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>), the algorithmic design of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and its implementation challenges (Section <a href="#S1.SS2" title="1.2 Background ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>), and how we address these challenges and go beyond (Section <a href="#S1.SS3" title="1.3 Our Results ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To formally justify the quadratic time complexity, we present the mathematical formulation of the exact attention computation. The <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q,K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q,K)</annotation></semantics></math>-softmax-attention matrix <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> captures relationships between each row of the query and the key using the inner product <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i,j}:=\exp\left(\tfrac{1}{d}\left\langle Q_{i,*},K_{j,*}\right\rangle\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i,j}:=\exp\left(\tfrac{1}{d}\left\langle Q_{i,*},K_{j,*}\right\rangle\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[n]:=\{1,2,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[n]:=\{1,2,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i,*},K_{j,*}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i,*},K_{j,*}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are the <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th rows of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The inner product <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\langle Q_{i,*},K_{j,*}\right\rangle=\|Q_{i,*}\|_{2}\|K_{j,*}\|_{2}\cos\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\langle Q_{i,*},K_{j,*}\right\rangle=\|Q_{i,*}\|_{2}\|K_{j,*}\|_{2}\cos\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, computed in <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> time, quantifies the similarity and alignment between <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i,*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i,*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{j,*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{j,*}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the entrywise exponential function further accentuates these relationships by magnifying large similarities and suppressing small ones. It is formally defined as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(Q,K\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(Q,K\right)</annotation></semantics></math>-softmax-attention matrix)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(Q,K\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(Q,K\right)</annotation></semantics></math>-softmax-attention matrix <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:=\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:=\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We further make each row of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> form a probability distribution by dividing each entry by the corresponding row sum. Finally, contextual information is aggregated by multiplying the normalized attention matrix with the value matrix <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. The exact softmax attention computation problem is defined as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(The exact attention computation)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K,V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K,V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(Q,K\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(Q,K\right)</annotation></semantics></math>-softmax-attention matrix (Definition <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 1.1 ((𝑄,𝐾)-softmax-attention matrix). ‣ 1.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>). For all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diag:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diag:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diag\left(x\right)_{i,j}:=x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diag\left(x\right)_{i,j}:=x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=j</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diag\left(x\right)_{i,j}:=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diag\left(x\right)_{i,j}:=0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D:=\diag\left(A\mathbf{1}_{n}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D:=\diag\left(A\mathbf{1}_{n}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an all <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> vector. The exact attention computation problem <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Att}\left(Q,K,V\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Att</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Att}\left(Q,K,V\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Att}\left(Q,K,V\right):=D^{-1}AV." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Att</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Att}\left(Q,K,V\right):=D^{-1}AV.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Background</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To reduce the quadratic time complexity <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{2}d\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{2}d\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> studies approximate attention computation to avoid fully constructing <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(The approximate attention computation)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be the accuracy parameter. For all matrix <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|M\right\|_{\infty}:=\max_{i,j}|M_{i,j}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|M\right\|_{\infty}:=\max_{i,j}|M_{i,j}|</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K,V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K,V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the goal of the approximate attention computation is to output a matrix <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> with:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|P-D^{-1}AV\right\|_{\infty}\leq\epsilon\cdot\|V\|_{\infty}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|P-D^{-1}AV\right\|_{\infty}\leq\epsilon\cdot\|V\|_{\infty}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> presents a time-optimal algorithm that approximates attention computation with small error:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Upper bound <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite></span>: if <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|Q\right\|_{\infty},\left\|K\right\|_{\infty},\left\|V\right\|_{\infty}\leq o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|Q\right\|_{\infty},\left\|K\right\|_{\infty},\left\|V\right\|_{\infty}\leq o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists a sub-quadratic time algorithm (Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ A.1.2 Background: Approximating the Attention Computation Assuming 𝑑=𝑂(log𝑛) and Bounded Entries ‣ A.1 Background: Technique Overview of the Polynomial Method ‣ Appendix A PRELIMINARIES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) that solves the approximate attention computation problem with <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=1/\poly\left(n\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=1/\poly\left(n\right)</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lower bound <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite></span>: if <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|Q\right\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|Q\right\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|K\right\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|K\right\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|V\right\|_{\infty}\geq\Omega\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|V\right\|_{\infty}\geq\Omega\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, then there does not exist any sub-quadratic time algorithm approximating attention computation under the Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (SETH, see Definition <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem6" title="Definition A.6 (Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (SETH), Hypothesis 4.1 in []). ‣ A.1.3 Background: Main Results of [] ‣ A.1 Background: Technique Overview of the Polynomial Method ‣ Appendix A PRELIMINARIES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a>) with <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=1/\poly\left(n\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=1/\poly\left(n\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01643v1/QK_10.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/333;" width="476" height="333"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Distribution of entries in the query and key matrices of Layer 10 in TinyLlama-1.1B.
The red dashed lines mark the thresholds <math id="S1.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm\sqrt{\log n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm\sqrt{\log n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Distributions of various other layers and transformer models are deferred to the Appendix <a href="#A13" title="Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain a fast algorithm for approximating attention computation, all entries in the matrices <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K,V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K,V</annotation></semantics></math> must be very small, i.e., <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\left\{\left\|Q\right\|_{\infty},\left\|K\right\|_{\infty},\left\|V\right\|_{\infty}\right\}\leq o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\left\{\left\|Q\right\|_{\infty},\left\|K\right\|_{\infty},\left\|V\right\|_{\infty}\right\}\leq o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. However, this bounded-entry assumption rarely holds in modern Transformer architectures (see Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1.2 Background ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Therefore, it is natural to ask:</p>
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can we accurately and efficiently approximate the attention computation with a practical assumption?</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>Our Results</h3>

<section id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contribution 1—An accurate, efficient, and implementable algorithm.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we provide a positive answer to this question. Our “practical assumption” stems from the fact that the entries of the query, key, and value matrices resemble a sub-Gaussian distribution (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1.2 Background ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). These entries typically cluster near the mean, and the number of extreme entries—those whose absolute values exceed <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>—is small.
After the first few iterations, the entries of <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> become tightly concentrated with light tails (see figures in Appendix <a href="#A13" title="Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M</span></a> for empirical support, where we exemplify the sub-Gaussianity on transformer architectures such as TinyLlama-1.1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>, LLaDA-8B-Base <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, OPT-1.3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite>, and Phi-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> across multiple layers).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Under this practical assumption, we decompose the query matrix <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> into the sum of a sparse matrix with large entries, <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a dense matrix with small entries, <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(s\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(s\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and apply the same decomposition to the key matrix <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. We then perform the exact computation for the sparse components <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and use the polynomial method of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> to approximate the remaining dense component <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. We call this approach <em id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single-threshold support basis decomposition</em>, where “single-threshold” denotes <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and “support basis” informally refers to constructing the disjoint matrices <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(s\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(s\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Definition <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition B.5 (Support basis). ‣ B.2 Support Basis ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a> for formal definition).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Informal version of Theorem <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem D.4 (Main Theorem Under Sub-Gaussian Assumption). ‣ D.4 Approximating the Attention Computation Under the Sub-Gaussian Assumption ‣ Appendix D ATTENTION OPTIMIZATION UNDER THE SUB-GAUSSIAN DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.4</span></a>)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K,V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K,V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the query, key, and value matrices. Suppose entries of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K</annotation></semantics></math> are independent and sub-Gaussian with variance proxies <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{Q}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{Q}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{K}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{K}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon,\delta\in(0,0.1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon,\delta\in(0,0.1)</annotation></semantics></math> respectively be the accuracy parameter and failure probability. Then, with probability at least <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a sub-quadratic time algorithm (Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Conquer: Approximate the attention in 𝐴^(𝐿) sparsity time. ‣ 3.2 Single-Threshold Support Basis ‣ 3 TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) that outputs <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfing</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|P-D^{-1}AV\right\|_{\infty}\leq\epsilon\cdot\|V\|_{\infty}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|P-D^{-1}AV\right\|_{\infty}\leq\epsilon\cdot\|V\|_{\infty},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\exp(QK^{\top}/d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\exp(QK^{\top}/d)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\mathrm{diag}(A\cdot\mathbf{1}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\mathrm{diag}(A\cdot\mathbf{1}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To the best of our knowledge, the matrices <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in all existing Transformer architectures (TinyLlama-1.1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>, LLaDA-8B-Base <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, OPT-1.3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite>, and Phi-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>) resemble this sub-Gaussian distribution, which implies that our sub-quadratic time randomized algorithm can be applied to all of them. However, it is also natural to ask:</p>
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can we generalize our support basis to any arbitrary <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> without any assumption?</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contribution 2—An efficient attention approximation algorithm with no assumption.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We again provide a positive answer to this question. Without any assumptions, the entries of <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> may not cluster near the mean, so we cannot infer that the number of large entries is small. Therefore, we develop <em id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multiple-threshold support basis</em> to approximate the attention computation in sub-quadratic time. However, due to the <em id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lower bound</em> result of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, this unavoidably leads to a weaker accuracy guarantee <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;1/\poly(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;1/\poly(n)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Informal version of Theorem <a href="#A9.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem I.5. ‣ I.5 Main Result ‣ Appendix I ATTENTION OPTIMIZATION VIA MULTI-THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS AND SKETCHING ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I.5</span></a>)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K,V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K,V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the query, key, and value matrices. Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon,\delta\in(0,0.1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon,\delta\in(0,0.1)</annotation></semantics></math> respectively be the accuracy parameter and failure probability. Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\max\{\|Q\|_{\infty},\|K\|_{\infty}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\max\{\|Q\|_{\infty},\|K\|_{\infty}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=\min_{i\in[n],j\in[d]}\{Q_{i,j},K_{i,j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=\min_{i\in[n],j\in[d]}\{Q_{i,j},K_{i,j}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, with probability <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the approximate attention computation (Definition <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 1.3 (The approximate attention computation). ‣ 1.2 Background ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>) can be solved in sub-quadratic time by outputting <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|P-D^{-1}AV\right\|_{\infty}\lesssim\epsilon\exp(B^{2}-2b^{2})\cdot\left\|V\right\|_{\infty}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|P-D^{-1}AV\right\|_{\infty}\lesssim\epsilon\exp(B^{2}-2b^{2})\cdot\left\|V\right\|_{\infty}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond the optimization contribution of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5 (Informal version of Theorem ). ‣ Contribution 2—An efficient attention approximation algorithm with no assumption. ‣ 1.3 Our Results ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>, our result also provides a theoretical justification for why polynomial attention performs well in practice. While <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> proposes a fast algorithm to approximate a polynomial attention and empirically demonstrates its effectiveness on downstream tasks, a theoretical explanation for this success remains lacking.</p>
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Why does the polynomial attention have comparable performance with the softmax attention?</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contribution 3—A theoretical justification for the empirical performance of polynomial attention and beyond.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our theorem, we bridge this gap by showing that softmax attention is <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to one polynomial attention, and this polynomial attention can, in turn, be approximated using the sketching methods of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> with accuracy <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that this polynomial attention is actually <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon_{1}+\epsilon_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon_{1}+\epsilon_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>-close to softmax attention, which explains why it achieves comparable experimental results, as shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, we note that the attention approximation method of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> does not perform well when the entries of <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> are far apart. This motivates us to design a <em id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multiple-threshold support basis</em>, which partitions <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> into several disjoint components. For the partitions with larger entries, instead of performing exact computation as in the <em id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single-threshold support basis</em>—whose running time becomes large without the sub-Gaussian assumption—we approximate them via a sum of polynomial attentions.
These theoretical findings not only support the empirical results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, but also go further: they demonstrate that combining multiple polynomial attentions yields significantly smaller <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> errors with softmax attention compared to using a single polynomial attention.
Thus, combining multiple polynomial attentions better captures the behavior of softmax attention than a single one in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contribution 4—Experimental results.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Besides empirically validating our assumption that the entries of query and key matrices resemble sub-Gaussian distributions, we also present runtime experiments showing that our method is faster than exact attention computation and achieves significantly lower error compared with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Additionally, we evaluate downstream tasks using both <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and our method across different benchmarks: <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> attains 0% accuracy, but our method achieves performance comparable to exact attention. However, since our paper is primarily theoretical, we defer the experimental results to Appendix <a href="#A11" title="Appendix K EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">K</span></a> and <a href="#A13" title="Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notation.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Z_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>ℤ</mo><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Z_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of positive integers. For any set <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|Y|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|Y|</annotation></semantics></math> to denote its cardinality. We define the combination <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{r}:=\frac{n!}{(n-r)!r!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{r}:=\frac{n!}{(n-r)!r!}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For all <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\circ y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\circ y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x\circ y)_{i}:=x_{i}\cdot y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x\circ y)_{i}:=x_{i}\cdot y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>; for all <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\circ B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\circ B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A\circ B)_{i,j}:=A_{i,j}\cdot B_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A\circ B)_{i,j}:=A_{i,j}\cdot B_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For all <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> being a positive integer, we define the <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> norm of <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|_{p}:=\left(\sum_{i\in[d]}|x_{i}|^{p}\right)^{1/p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|_{p}:=\left(\sum_{i\in[d]}|x_{i}|^{p}\right)^{1/p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\circ p}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\circ p}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A^{\circ p})_{i,j}:=A_{i,j}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A^{\circ p})_{i,j}:=A_{i,j}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\supp(A):=\{(i,j)\in[n]\times[d]\mid A_{i,j}\neq 0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\supp(A):=\{(i,j)\in[n]\times[d]\mid A_{i,j}\neq 0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf 1}_{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf 1}_{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> as an <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times d</annotation></semantics></math> matrix whose entries are all <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>’s.
For a probability space <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\Pr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Pr</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\Pr)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> is the sample space, <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra of events, and <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr:\mathcal{F}\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr:\mathcal{F}\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is the probability measure, we define the discrete random variable <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> so that the expectation of <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m31" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\E[X]:=\sum_{i}x_{i}\cdot\Pr[X=x_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝔼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\E[X]:=\sum_{i}x_{i}\cdot\Pr[X=x_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Roadmap.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 RELATED WORK ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we present the related work. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we give an overview of all the techniques we use to prove our main results (Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4 (Informal version of Theorem ). ‣ Contribution 1—An accurate, efficient, and implementable algorithm. ‣ 1.3 Our Results ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5 (Informal version of Theorem ). ‣ Contribution 2—An efficient attention approximation algorithm with no assumption. ‣ 1.3 Our Results ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>RELATED WORK</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Polynomial methods for attention approximation.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As the attention approximation algorithm of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> is time optimal—its upper bound on runtime matching the lower bound—it has inspired numerous follow-up works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>. They generalize the polynomial method from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> to establish upper and lower bounds for various attention-approximation–related problems. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> extends this method to study tensor attention inference, showing that the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-th order tensor attention can only be approximated in nearly linear time under an even stronger assumption: <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\left\{\left\|Q\right\|_{\infty},\left\|K\right\|_{\infty},\left\|V\right\|_{\infty}\right\}\leq o\left(\sqrt[p]{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mroot><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mroot><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\left\{\left\|Q\right\|_{\infty},\left\|K\right\|_{\infty},\left\|V\right\|_{\infty}\right\}\leq o\left(\sqrt[p]{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> proves that the gradient of attention can be approximated in nearly linear time, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> shows the same for the gradient of tensor attention. Moreover, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> apply the method of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> to RoPE
attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> inference and training, respectively. All of these works rely on the strict bounded-entry assumption and contain no experimental results. Although our method is specifically built upon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, it can be generalized to all these works to eliminate the bounded-entry assumption, since they all rely on similar techniques.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A recent work by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> develops upon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> by finding the relationships between the values of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and different hidden dimensions <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. They show that when <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and the entries of the input matrices are bounded by <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\poly(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\poly(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, the approximate attention computation problem admits a truly subquadratic-time algorithm with runtime <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(n^{2-1/d}\cdot\poly\log(B/\varepsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(n^{2-1/d}\cdot\poly\log(B/\varepsilon))</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, when <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2^{\Theta(\log^{*}n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2^{\Theta(\log^{*}n)}</annotation></semantics></math>, they establish a conditional lower bound under the SETH, proving that any algorithm computing the approximate attention computation problem must take <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{2-o(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{2-o(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> time. Finally, in the regime where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\poly(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\poly(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, they show that the standard algorithm with runtime <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{2}d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{2}d)</annotation></semantics></math> is conditionally optimal. Although <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> gives a more flexible choice of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, it is still unclear how we can design a fast algorithm approximating the attention computation problem if there are entries in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K,V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K,V</annotation></semantics></math> larger than <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Other attention optimization works.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Other attention approximation works also rely on different types of assumptions that are incomparable to our work. For instance, Reformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> introduces Locality-Sensitive Hashing (LSH) Attention, which reduces the computational complexity from <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n\log n\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n\log n\right)</annotation></semantics></math> by assuming that, for each query vector, only a small subset of the nearest key vectors substantially contributes to the softmax output. Similarly, Longformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> and Big Bird <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite> build on the sparse attention matrix assumption.
Linear Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> replaces the softmax operation with a kernel-based formulation, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> replaces the entrywise exponential with an entrywise polynomial. Linformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite> assumes the self-attention matrix is low-rank.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The most similar work to our <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multiple-threshold support basis</em> result is <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, which is also motivated by alleviating strict assumptions in prior works and develops a sub-quadratic time algorithm for attention approximation with a causal mask. However, their approach is limited to the case where the causal mask is a lower-triangular matrix in which all lower-triangular entries are equal to 1. Consequently, when applied with this mask, the attention matrix also becomes lower triangular. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> decomposes the masked attention into a set of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> “conv bases” with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> and analyzes each basis in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n\log n)</annotation></semantics></math> time via Fast Fourier Transform to achieve sub-quadratic runtime. While this is similar in spirit to our approach, our task is significantly more challenging: we decompose a dense <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> attention matrix with no zero entries into a sum of disjoint components and analyze each using the polynomial method and sketching. Both works share the advantage of tuning the number of bases, but our method is more promising because our bucketing scheme generates at most a logarithmic number of bases. In contrast, the number of bases in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> can be as large as <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in the worst case, which yields no improvement in runtime.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our theoretical proofs are deferred to the Appendix. Now, we summarize the methods used in our proofs to support our main results.
In Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Background: The Origin of the Bounded Entry Assumption ‣ 3 TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we summarize the polynomial method from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> to show the cause of the bounded entry assumption. In Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Single-Threshold Support Basis ‣ 3 TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we show that our single-threshold support basis can “remove” this bounded entry assumption, proving Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4 (Informal version of Theorem ). ‣ Contribution 1—An accurate, efficient, and implementable algorithm. ‣ 1.3 Our Results ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>. In Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Multiple-Threshold Support Basis ‣ 3 TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, we present our multiple-threshold support bases to prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5 (Informal version of Theorem ). ‣ Contribution 2—An efficient attention approximation algorithm with no assumption. ‣ 1.3 Our Results ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Background: The Origin of the Bounded Entry Assumption</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Accuracy-Efficiency trade-off.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before delving into our method, we first provide an overview of the techniques used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> to explain the origin of the bounded entry assumption. The attention approximation (Definition <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 1.3 (The approximate attention computation). ‣ 1.2 Background ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>) can be computed in sub-quadratic time by replacing the entry-wise exponential function <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> in the softmax unit with a degree-<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> Chebyshev polynomial <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby reducing the time complexity. This polynomial is also applied entry-wisely to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}/d\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}/d\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each entry <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle Q_{i,*},K_{j,*}\rangle/d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle Q_{i,*},K_{j,*}\rangle/d</annotation></semantics></math> is the inner product between a query and a key vector. When this polynomial is expanded, it produces a collection of monomials that are products of certain entries from <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. These terms can be reorganized into a low-rank (rank-<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>) factorization of the form <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}U_{2}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}U_{2}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\binom{2d+2g}{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\binom{2d+2g}{2d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similar as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, we give the following example to approximate the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>-th entry of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~p\left(\langle Q_{i,*},K_{j,*}\rangle/d\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~p\left(\langle Q_{i,*},K_{j,*}\rangle/d\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~p\left(\frac{1}{d}\sum_{\ell=1}^{d}Q_{i,\ell}\cdot K_{j,\ell}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~p\left(\frac{1}{d}\sum_{\ell=1}^{d}Q_{i,\ell}\cdot K_{j,\ell}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~3Q_{i,1}K_{j,1}Q_{i,3}K_{j,3}-4Q_{i,7}K_{j,7}Q_{i,16}^{3}K_{j,16}^{3}+\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~3Q_{i,1}K_{j,1}Q_{i,3}K_{j,3}-4Q_{i,7}K_{j,7}Q_{i,16}^{3}K_{j,16}^{3}+\dots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\left\langle\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}3Q_{i,1}Q_{i,3}\\
-4Q_{i,7}Q_{i,16}^{3}\\
\vdots\end{bmatrix}}_{\left(U_{1}\right)_{i,*}},\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}K_{j,1}K_{j,3}\\
K_{j,7}K_{j,16}^{3}\\
\vdots\end{bmatrix}}_{\left(U_{2}\right)_{j,*}}\right\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub></munder><mo>,</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub></munder></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\left\langle\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}3Q_{i,1}Q_{i,3}\\
-4Q_{i,7}Q_{i,16}^{3}\\
\vdots\end{bmatrix}}_{\left(U_{1}\right)_{i,*}},\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}K_{j,1}K_{j,3}\\
K_{j,7}K_{j,16}^{3}\\
\vdots\end{bmatrix}}_{\left(U_{2}\right)_{j,*}}\right\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\left(U_{1}\right)_{i,*}^{\top}\cdot\left(U_{2}\right)_{j,*}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\left(U_{1}\right)_{i,*}^{\top}\cdot\left(U_{2}\right)_{j,*}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01643v1/poly_method.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/139;" width="476" height="139"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>A visualization of the polynomial method. For simplicity, we set <math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The matrix <math id="S3.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> has rank 1, but the entrywise exponential function <math id="S3.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>exp</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp</annotation></semantics></math> may map it to a full-rank matrix. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> shows that replacing <math id="S3.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>exp</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp</annotation></semantics></math> with a degree-<math id="S3.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> Chebyshev polynomial <math id="S3.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> can produce a matrix of rank <math id="S3.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For all <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, to accurately approximate <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(x)</annotation></semantics></math> on the range <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-B,B]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-B,B]</annotation></semantics></math>, the degree <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> of the polynomial must grow with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> (see Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma A.4 (An improved version of Lemma 3.4 in []). ‣ A.1.2 Background: Approximating the Attention Computation Assuming 𝑑=𝑂(log𝑛) and Bounded Entries ‣ A.1 Background: Technique Overview of the Polynomial Method ‣ Appendix A PRELIMINARIES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> for details). This leads to an accuracy–efficiency trade-off: increasing <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> improves the approximation accuracy, but also increases the rank <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\binom{2d+2g}{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\binom{2d+2g}{2d}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby slowing down the computation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Time complexity for constructing <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> and approximating the attention computation.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now explain why constructing <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> takes <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(nrg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(nrg)</annotation></semantics></math> time and using them to approximate the attention computation takes <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(nrd)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(nrd)</annotation></semantics></math> time. As shown in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In Accuracy-Efficiency trade-off. ‣ 3.1 Background: The Origin of the Bounded Entry Assumption ‣ 3 TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), each degree-<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> Chebyshev polynomial may give one row of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{1})_{i,*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{1})_{i,*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and one row of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{2})_{j,*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{2})_{j,*}</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, we need <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> numbers of Chebyshev polynomials to fully construct <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each polynomial expansion contains <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> monomials, so each row vector <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{1})_{i,*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{1})_{i,*}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{2})_{j,*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{2})_{j,*}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> entries to be computed (see from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In Accuracy-Efficiency trade-off. ‣ 3.1 Background: The Origin of the Bounded Entry Assumption ‣ 3 TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) to Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In Accuracy-Efficiency trade-off. ‣ 3.1 Background: The Origin of the Bounded Entry Assumption ‣ 3 TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) as an example).
Additionally, if the polynomial is degree-<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, computing the value of each entry of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{1})_{i,*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{1})_{i,*}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{2})_{j,*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{2})_{j,*}</annotation></semantics></math> involves multiplying up to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(g)</annotation></semantics></math> scalar factors, i.e., entries from <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (see entries in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In Accuracy-Efficiency trade-off. ‣ 3.1 Background: The Origin of the Bounded Entry Assumption ‣ 3 TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) as an example). Thus, each monomial evaluation costs <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(g)</annotation></semantics></math> scalar multiplications. Since we compute <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> rows, each with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> monomials, and each monomial costs <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(g)</annotation></semantics></math> operations, constructing <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> requires <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(nrg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(nrg)</annotation></semantics></math> time.
Now, we do not need to explicitly form the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> attention matrix <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)</annotation></semantics></math> but instead work with the much smaller factors <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\approx U_{1}U_{2}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\approx U_{1}U_{2}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, using the associativity of matrix multiplication, the attention computation (as in Definition <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 1.2 (The exact attention computation). ‣ 1.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>)</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\diag\left(\underbrace{U_{1}}_{n\times r}\left(\underbrace{U_{2}^{\top}}_{r\times n}\underbrace{{\bf 1}_{n}}_{n\times 1}\right)\right)^{-1}\underbrace{U_{1}}_{n\times r}\left(\underbrace{U_{2}^{\top}}_{r\times n}\underbrace{V}_{n\times d}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\diag\left(\underbrace{U_{1}}_{n\times r}\left(\underbrace{U_{2}^{\top}}_{r\times n}\underbrace{{\bf 1}_{n}}_{n\times 1}\right)\right)^{-1}\underbrace{U_{1}}_{n\times r}\left(\underbrace{U_{2}^{\top}}_{r\times n}\underbrace{V}_{n\times d}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">can be approximated in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(nrd\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(nrd\right)</annotation></semantics></math> time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To approximate the attention in nearly linear time, we need to make <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(nrg+nrd)=O(n^{1+o(1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(nrg+nrd)=O(n^{1+o(1)})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is equivalent to making <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d,g,r\leq n^{o(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d,g,r\leq n^{o(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, keeping <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> small requires <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> (the maximum absolute entry of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>) to be small. The analysis in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> shows that achieving both a fast runtime and a small approximation error requires <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=O(\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=O(\log n)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. This condition on <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is known as the bounded entry assumption.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Single-Threshold Support Basis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To eliminate the bounded entry assumption, the most straightforward approach is to scan all entries of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and move those larger than the threshold <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The matrices containing the remaining smaller entries are denoted <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(s\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(s\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\left(s\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\left(s\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote this process as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Split}(Q,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Split</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Split}(Q,T)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Split}(K,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Split</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Split}(K,T)</annotation></semantics></math>. We perform exact computation for the terms related to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and use the <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> polynomial method to approximate <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, we notice that since <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>exp</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp</annotation></semantics></math> is applied entry-wisely, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\exp\left(A+B\right)\right)\cdot V=\left(\exp\left(A\right)\circ\exp\left(B\right)\right)\cdot V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\exp\left(A+B\right)\right)\cdot V=\left(\exp\left(A\right)\circ\exp\left(B\right)\right)\cdot V</annotation></semantics></math>, which cannot be further simplified as multiplication <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math> is not distributive over Hadamard product <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circ</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, we cannot divide and conquer it using this naive approach.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Divide: Design a valid support basis.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our technique is to design a novel decomposition method that breaks down <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}/d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}/d</annotation></semantics></math> into a sum of <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">disjoint matrices</em>—For a set of matrices <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A_{i}\}_{i\in[m]}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A_{i}\}_{i\in[m]}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\Z_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">∈</mo><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em">ℤ</mo><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\Z_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be a disjoint matrix if for all <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(j,k\right)\in[n]\times[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(j,k\right)\in[n]\times[d]</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\prime}\neq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}\neq i</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(A_{i}\right)_{j,k}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(A_{i}\right)_{j,k}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(A_{i^{\prime}}\right)_{j,k}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(A_{i^{\prime}}\right)_{j,k}=0</annotation></semantics></math> (see Definition <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition B.1 (Disjoint matrices). ‣ B.1 Disjoint Matrices and Their Properties ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> for details). With two disjoint matrices <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(s\right)},A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(s\right)},A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}=Q^{(L)}K^{\top}+Q^{(s)}\left(K^{(L)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{(s)}\left(K^{(s)}\right)^{\top}=A^{\left(s\right)}+A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}=Q^{(L)}K^{\top}+Q^{(s)}\left(K^{(L)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{(s)}\left(K^{(s)}\right)^{\top}=A^{\left(s\right)}+A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A^{\left(s\right)},A^{\left(L\right)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A^{\left(s\right)},A^{\left(L\right)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> as a <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">support basis</em> of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In our work, we construct <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(s\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(s\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> as a dense matrix, and all of its entries are bounded by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>; we construct <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> as a sparse matrix, and its entries can be large.
Therefore, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(s\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(s\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> is suitable for approximation using the <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> polynomial method, whereas <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> is suitable to be computed exactly since its sparsity ensures the fast matrix multiplication. Moreover, this decomposition has a desirable mathematical property under the entry-wise exponential function <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>exp</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown in Fact <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem2" title="Fact B.2. ‣ B.1 Disjoint Matrices and Their Properties ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)=\exp\left(A^{\left(s\right)}/d\right)+\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)=\exp\left(A^{\left(s\right)}/d\right)+\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This additive form is essential for the divide-and-conquer strategy, as multiplication <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math> is distributive over addition <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>. This can break the original attention computation problem <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{-1}\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{-1}\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)V</annotation></semantics></math> into a sum of two simpler problems <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{-1}\exp\left(A^{\left(s\right)}/d\right)V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{-1}\exp\left(A^{\left(s\right)}/d\right)V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{-1}\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{-1}\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)V</annotation></semantics></math>. Before presenting how we conquer each of them, we first present how to construct such a valid support basis <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A^{\left(s\right)},A^{\left(L\right)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A^{\left(s\right)},A^{\left(L\right)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. All of the entries of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(L\right)}K^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(L\right)}K^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> are “potentially large”, so they should be included in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. To further ensure that our current <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> is disjoint from the remaining dense component <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(i,j\right)\in[n]\times[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(i,j\right)\in[n]\times[n]</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}K^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}K^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we extract the corresponding entry <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and add it into <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(A^{\left(L\right)}\right)_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(A^{\left(L\right)}\right)_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Regarding <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(s\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(s\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(A^{\left(s\right)}\right)_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(A^{\left(s\right)}\right)_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(A^{\left(L\right)}\right)_{i,j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(A^{\left(L\right)}\right)_{i,j}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise. Then, this forms a valid support basis.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01643v1/AL_construction.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/180;" width="476" height="180"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>A visualization of <math id="S3.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}V</annotation></semantics></math>. By the definition of <math id="S3.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\supp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}\right)=\supp\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}K^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\supp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}\right)=\supp\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}K^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, visualizing <math id="S3.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}V</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to visualize <math id="S3.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}K^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)\right)V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}K^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)\right)V</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition, <math id="S3.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(L\right)},K^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(L\right)},K^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> are sparse matrices with <math id="S3.F3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\supp\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\right)\right|=\left|\supp\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)\right|=O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\supp\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\right)\right|=\left|\supp\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)\right|=O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.F3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\left(0,1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\left(0,1\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, so there are at most <math id="S3.F3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> non-zero rows (red blocks) in <math id="S3.F3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(L\right)}K^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(L\right)}K^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> non-zero columns (blue blocks) in <math id="S3.F3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conquer: Approximate the attention in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> sparsity time.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we have shown that we can construct a support basis <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A^{\left(s\right)},A^{\left(L\right)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A^{\left(s\right)},A^{\left(L\right)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> to make attention computation be divided into the sum of two smaller problems: <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{-1}\exp\left(A^{\left(s\right)}/d\right)V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{-1}\exp\left(A^{\left(s\right)}/d\right)V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{-1}\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{-1}\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)V</annotation></semantics></math>. Below, we justify the statement that if the number of large entries (those greater than <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>) of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is upper bounded by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\left(0,1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\left(0,1\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the attention computation can be approximated in sub-quadratic time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To conquer each of these smaller problems, we efficiently approximate the former using <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> polynomial method and exactly compute the latter. To ensure that our attention approximation algorithm runs in sub-quadratic time, it suffices to make the exact computation part sub-quadratic, since the polynomial method always takes <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{1+o\left(1\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{1+o\left(1\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> time. Recall that we define <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(A^{\left(L\right)}\right)_{i,j}:=\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(A^{\left(L\right)}\right)_{i,j}:=\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> if
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}K^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}K^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise. Since <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, computing an arbitrary entry of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> only takes <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> time. Thus, we need to know how many of the entries of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> are non-zero.
As the number of large entries of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is at most <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\left(0,1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\left(0,1\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(s\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(s\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> to be dense and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(L\right)},K^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(L\right)},K^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> to be sparse. Thus, the support of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented as in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Divide: Design a valid support basis. ‣ 3.2 Single-Threshold Support Basis ‣ 3 TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We get <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{supp}\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)=\mathrm{supp}\left(A^{\left(L\right)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{supp}\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)=\mathrm{supp}\left(A^{\left(L\right)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp\left(0\right)-1=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp\left(0\right)-1=0</annotation></semantics></math>. When computing <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)V</annotation></semantics></math>, it suffices to consider:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1</span>: for some rows (red blocks in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Divide: Design a valid support basis. ‣ 3.2 Single-Threshold Support Basis ‣ 3 TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)V</annotation></semantics></math>, we need to compute <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> numbers of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle a,b\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle a,b\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are dense; this takes <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nd</annotation></semantics></math> time. There are <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> numbers of such rows, so in total, it takes <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{1+\alpha}d\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{1+\alpha}d\right)</annotation></semantics></math> time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2</span>: for others (blue blocks in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Divide: Design a valid support basis. ‣ 3.2 Single-Threshold Support Basis ‣ 3 TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), we need to compute <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> numbers of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle a^{\prime},b\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle a^{\prime},b\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> sparse and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is dense; this takes <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{\alpha}d\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{\alpha}d\right)</annotation></semantics></math> time. There are at most <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> numbers of such rows, so in total, it takes <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{1+\alpha}d\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{1+\alpha}d\right)</annotation></semantics></math> time.
Combining both cases together, with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=O\left(\log n\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=O\left(\log n\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, it takes <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{1+\alpha}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{1+\alpha}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> time to compute <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)V</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{1+\alpha}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{1+\alpha}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is the sparsity of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> (Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma B.9. ‣ B.4 Running Time and Sparsity Analysis ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a>), we call it <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> sparsity time.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> We approximate the attention computation problem using the support basis <math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A^{\left(s\right)},A^{\left(L\right)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A^{\left(s\right)},A^{\left(L\right)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">procedure</span> <span id="alg1.l1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ApproxAttention</span>(<math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Q\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d},K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d},V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d},n,d,\epsilon,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d},K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d},V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d},n,d,\epsilon,T</annotation></semantics></math>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(L\right)},Q^{\left(s\right)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\leftarrow\textsc{Split}(Q,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Split</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(L\right)},Q^{\left(s\right)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\leftarrow\textsc{Split}(Q,T)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\left(L\right)},K^{\left(s\right)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\leftarrow\textsc{Split}(K,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Split</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\left(L\right)},K^{\left(s\right)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\leftarrow\textsc{Split}(K,T)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
                Explicitly compute <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. 

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}\leftarrow\textsc{Polynomial}(A^{\left(s\right)},\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Polynomial</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}\leftarrow\textsc{Polynomial}(A^{\left(s\right)},\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> To approximate <math id="alg1.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp\left(A^{\left(s\right)}/d\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp\left(A^{\left(s\right)}/d\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. 
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
                
                
                Get <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}\leftarrow U_{1}\left(U_{2}^{\top}{\bf 1}_{n}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}\leftarrow U_{1}\left(U_{2}^{\top}{\bf 1}_{n}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. 

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
                
                
                Get <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}\leftarrow\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right){\bf 1}_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}\leftarrow\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right){\bf 1}_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. 

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
                
                
                Get <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{-1}\leftarrow\diag\left(d_{1}+d_{2}\right)^{-1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{-1}\leftarrow\diag\left(d_{1}+d_{2}\right)^{-1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>. 

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
                
                
                Get <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}\leftarrow U_{1}\left(U_{2}^{\top}V\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}\leftarrow U_{1}\left(U_{2}^{\top}V\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. 

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
                
                
                Get <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}\leftarrow\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}\leftarrow\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. 

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{-1}(C_{1}+C_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{-1}(C_{1}+C_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. 

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">procedure</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Constructing <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\diag\left(\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right){\bf 1}_{n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\diag\left(\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right){\bf 1}_{n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> also takes <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> sparsity time: because of the linearity property of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diag:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diag:\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the time complexity is dominated by computing <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right){\bf 1}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right){\bf 1}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which, similarly, takes <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{1+\alpha}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{1+\alpha}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> time. Finally, as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is diagonal, the attention computation problem can be approximated in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> sparsity time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sub-Gaussianity of query and key.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have now justified the statement that if the number of large entries (those greater than <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>) of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is upper bounded by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n^{\alpha}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\left(0,1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\left(0,1\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the attention computation can be approximated in sub-quadratic time. What remains is to justify why this “if” condition holds. This is where our assumption—that the entries of the query and key matrices follow a sub-Gaussian distribution—becomes crucial. Suppose <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> are random matrices with independent sub-Gaussian entries having variances <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{Q}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{Q}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{K}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{K}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, we have
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pr\left[\left|Q_{i,j}\right|\geq t\right]\leq 2\exp\left(-\frac{t^{2}}{\sigma_{Q}^{2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≥</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\left[\left|Q_{i,j}\right|\geq t\right]\leq 2\exp\left(-\frac{t^{2}}{\sigma_{Q}^{2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pr\left[\left|K_{i,j}\right|\geq t\right]\leq 2\exp\left(-\frac{t^{2}}{\sigma_{K}^{2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≥</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\left[\left|K_{i,j}\right|\geq t\right]\leq 2\exp\left(-\frac{t^{2}}{\sigma_{K}^{2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This implies that the expected number of large entries in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\left(L\right)}\in\{Q^{\left(L\right)},K^{\left(L\right)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\left(L\right)}\in\{Q^{\left(L\right)},K^{\left(L\right)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded as: <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\left|\mathrm{supp}\left(M^{\left(L\right)}\right)\right|\right]\leq 2nd\cdot\exp\left(-\frac{T^{2}}{\sigma_{M}^{2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\left|\mathrm{supp}\left(M^{\left(L\right)}\right)\right|\right]\leq 2nd\cdot\exp\left(-\frac{T^{2}}{\sigma_{M}^{2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying the multiplicative Chernoff bound, we obtain that with high probability, our “if” condition holds:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\Pr\left[\left|\mathrm{supp}\left(M^{\left(L\right)}\right)\right|&gt;n^{\alpha}\right]\leq\exp\left(-\Omega\left(n^{\alpha}\right)\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>supp</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">&gt;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Pr\left[\left|\mathrm{supp}\left(M^{\left(L\right)}\right)\right|&gt;n^{\alpha}\right]\leq\exp\left(-\Omega\left(n^{\alpha}\right)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Putting everything together, we conclude the proof sketch of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4 (Informal version of Theorem ). ‣ Contribution 1—An accurate, efficient, and implementable algorithm. ‣ 1.3 Our Results ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> by Modus Ponens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generalize to multi-layer attention</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Generalizing our method to multi-layer attention requires efficient algorithms for both inference and training. Inference can be achieved using Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Conquer: Approximate the attention in 𝐴^(𝐿) sparsity time. ‣ 3.2 Single-Threshold Support Basis ‣ 3 TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, while training involves efficiently approximating the gradient of the loss function
with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(X)=\diag\left(\exp(X_{\ell}XX_{\ell}^{\top}/d){\bf 1}_{n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(X)=\diag\left(\exp(X_{\ell}XX_{\ell}^{\top}/d){\bf 1}_{n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=W_{Q}W_{K}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=W_{Q}W_{K}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> is the variable, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the desired output. As noted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, similar to the inference case, the dominant term in the time complexity of computing <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}L}{\mathrm{d}X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}L}{\mathrm{d}X}</annotation></semantics></math> still arises from the terms related to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\exp(X_{\ell}XX_{\ell}^{\top}/d)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\exp(X_{\ell}XX_{\ell}^{\top}/d)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, replacing <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> with the low-rank matrix <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}U_{2}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}U_{2}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> like <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> can approximate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}L}{\mathrm{d}X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}L}{\mathrm{d}X}</annotation></semantics></math> in sub-quadratic time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, our method expresses <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp\left(A^{\left(s\right)}/d\right)+\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp\left(A^{\left(s\right)}/d\right)+\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In Divide: Design a valid support basis. ‣ 3.2 Single-Threshold Support Basis ‣ 3 TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)). Since the entries of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp\left(A^{\left(s\right)}/d\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp\left(A^{\left(s\right)}/d\right)</annotation></semantics></math> are small, we can apply the same technique as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> to approximate it by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}U_{2}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}U_{2}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. Although <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\exp\left(A^{\left(L\right)}/d\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is sparse, its product with other dense matrices is dense, and thus further computation may still require quadratic time <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\left(n^{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\left(n^{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
To address this issue, we use the approximate SVD from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, allowing us to approximate the best rank-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> low-rank approximation of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> sparsity time (Theorem <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem E.3 (Theorem 8.1 in []). ‣ Appendix E GENERALIZATION TO MULTI-LAYER TRANSFORMER ARCHITECTURES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a>). Choosing <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=n^{o(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=n^{o(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, we approximate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}L}{\mathrm{d}X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}L}{\mathrm{d}X}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> sparsity time. Thus, by sub-Gaussianity, we can approximate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}L}{\mathrm{d}X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}L}{\mathrm{d}X}</annotation></semantics></math> in sub-quadratic time, thereby generalizing our method to multi-layer attention.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Multiple-Threshold Support Basis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The single-threshold support basis works well when <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> have only a small number of “large” entries, which we can guarantee under the sub-Gaussian assumption. However, when we do <em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> make any distributional assumptions, we can no longer bound the number of large entries in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> using concentration inequalities. In this case, the exact computation on the large-entry component becomes too expensive.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Divide: Design a multiple-threshold support basis.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address it, we develop the <em id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multiple-threshold support basis</em> to decompose <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> into several disjoint components by a sequence of thresholds <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}&gt;T_{2}&gt;\cdots&gt;T_{m}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}&gt;T_{2}&gt;\cdots&gt;T_{m}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
These thresholds partition the range of entry magnitudes into <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint intervals. For example, entries within <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T_{\ell-1},T_{\ell}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T_{\ell-1},T_{\ell}]</annotation></semantics></math> are assigned to the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-th “bucket”. We write <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=Q^{(T_{1})}+Q^{(T_{2})}+\cdots+Q^{(T_{m})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=Q^{(T_{1})}+Q^{(T_{2})}+\cdots+Q^{(T_{m})}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=K^{(T_{1})}+K^{(T_{2})}+\cdots+K^{(T_{m})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=K^{(T_{1})}+K^{(T_{2})}+\cdots+K^{(T_{m})}</annotation></semantics></math>. To ensure the disjointness of each <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(T_{\ell},T_{\ell^{\prime}}\right)}:=Q^{\left(T_{\ell}\right)}\left(K^{\left(T_{\ell^{\prime}}\right)}\right)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(T_{\ell},T_{\ell^{\prime}}\right)}:=Q^{\left(T_{\ell}\right)}\left(K^{\left(T_{\ell^{\prime}}\right)}\right)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, if we find a large entry from <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in the interval <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T_{\ell-1},T_{\ell}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T_{\ell-1},T_{\ell}]</annotation></semantics></math>, we take the entire row containing this entry and include it in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{(T_{\ell})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{(T_{\ell})}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{(T_{\ell})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{(T_{\ell})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then expand the product <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> as
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}=\sum_{\ell=1}^{m}\sum_{\ell^{\prime}=1}^{m}A^{\left(T_{\ell},T_{\ell^{\prime}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}=\sum_{\ell=1}^{m}\sum_{\ell^{\prime}=1}^{m}A^{\left(T_{\ell},T_{\ell^{\prime}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since there are <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such matrices <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(T_{\ell},T_{\ell^{\prime}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(T_{\ell},T_{\ell^{\prime}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, we choose <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> to grow exponentially with respect to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> to keep <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> small.
Thus, we only need to handle a small number of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(T_{\ell},T_{\ell^{\prime}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(T_{\ell},T_{\ell^{\prime}})}</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve the desired running time.
Due to its disjointness, the support basis <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A^{\left(T_{l},T_{l^{\prime}}\right)}\}_{T_{l},T_{l^{\prime}}\in\{T_{\ell}\}_{\ell=1}^{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A^{\left(T_{l},T_{l^{\prime}}\right)}\}_{T_{l},T_{l^{\prime}}\in\{T_{\ell}\}_{\ell=1}^{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a desirable additive decomposition (by induction) analogous to Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In Divide: Design a valid support basis. ‣ 3.2 Single-Threshold Support Basis ‣ 3 TECHNIQUE OVERVIEW ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) for the single-threshold support basis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conquer: Reduce Gaussian kernels to polynomial kernels and solve them by oblivious sketching.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The largest entries in each of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{(T_{\ell})}(K^{(T_{\ell^{\prime}})})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{(T_{\ell})}(K^{(T_{\ell^{\prime}})})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> is at most <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\cdot T_{\ell}\cdot T_{\ell^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\cdot T_{\ell}\cdot T_{\ell^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, to make each of them satisfy the bounded entry assumption, we need to take out a scalar from this matrix <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{(T_{\ell})}(K^{(T_{\ell^{\prime}})})^{\top}=\frac{T_{\ell}\cdot T_{\ell^{\prime}}}{\log n}\cdot Q^{(\ell)}(K^{(\ell^{\prime})})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{(T_{\ell})}(K^{(T_{\ell^{\prime}})})^{\top}=\frac{T_{\ell}\cdot T_{\ell^{\prime}}}{\log n}\cdot Q^{(\ell)}(K^{(\ell^{\prime})})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> so that the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> norm of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{(\ell)}(K^{(\ell^{\prime})})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{(\ell)}(K^{(\ell^{\prime})})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> is small. As <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>exp</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp</annotation></semantics></math> is applied entry-wisely, we can get
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp\left(Q^{(T_{\ell})}(K^{(T_{\ell^{\prime}})})^{\top}\right)=\exp\left(Q^{(\ell)}(K^{(\ell^{\prime})})^{\top}\right)^{\circ\frac{T_{\ell}\cdot T_{\ell^{\prime}}}{\log n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp\left(Q^{(T_{\ell})}(K^{(T_{\ell^{\prime}})})^{\top}\right)=\exp\left(Q^{(\ell)}(K^{(\ell^{\prime})})^{\top}\right)^{\circ\frac{T_{\ell}\cdot T_{\ell^{\prime}}}{\log n}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where the power <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{T_{\ell}\cdot T_{\ell^{\prime}}}{\log n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{T_{\ell}\cdot T_{\ell^{\prime}}}{\log n}</annotation></semantics></math> is also applied entry-wisely on the matrix <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp\left(Q^{(\ell)}(K^{(\ell^{\prime})})^{\top}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp\left(Q^{(\ell)}(K^{(\ell^{\prime})})^{\top}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we can apply the <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> Chebyshev polynomial method to approximate <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp\left(Q^{(\ell)}(K^{(\ell^{\prime})})^{\top}\right)\approx U_{1}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\left(U_{2}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\right)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp\left(Q^{(\ell)}(K^{(\ell^{\prime})})^{\top}\right)\approx U_{1}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\left(U_{2}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\right)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> in nearly-linear time. We can finally approximate the polynomial attention
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{-1}\left(U_{1}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\left(U_{2}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)^{\circ\frac{T_{\ell}\cdot T_{\ell^{\prime}}}{\log n}}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{-1}\left(U_{1}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\left(U_{2}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)^{\circ\frac{T_{\ell}\cdot T_{\ell^{\prime}}}{\log n}}V</annotation></semantics></math>
in linear time in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> via oblivious sketching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, which provides a randomized low-dimensional embedding so that
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\langle\left(U_{1}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\right)_{i,*},\left(U_{2}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\right)_{j,*}\right\rangle^{\frac{T_{\ell}\cdot T_{\ell^{\prime}}}{\log n}}\approx\left\langle\phi^{\prime}\left(\left(U_{1}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\right)_{i,*}\right),\phi^{\prime}\left(\left(U_{2}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\right)_{j,*}\right)\right\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\langle\left(U_{1}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\right)_{i,*},\left(U_{2}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\right)_{j,*}\right\rangle^{\frac{T_{\ell}\cdot T_{\ell^{\prime}}}{\log n}}\approx\left\langle\phi^{\prime}\left(\left(U_{1}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\right)_{i,*}\right),\phi^{\prime}\left(\left(U_{2}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\right)_{j,*}\right)\right\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>
without explicitly computing the high dimensional inner product.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Error analysis.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The reason to use bucketing is to control the range of values over which we approximate <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Without bucketing, we would need to approximate <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(x)</annotation></semantics></math> over the entire range of entries in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}/d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}/d</annotation></semantics></math>, which may contain both very small and very large values. If this range is wide, then we must take out a large scalar, which leads to a very high-degree polynomial. This will greatly increase the error, as by the mean value theorem, we can show
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|A^{\circ p}-B^{\circ p}\right\|_{\infty}\leq p\cdot\beta^{p-1}\cdot\left\|A-B\right\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|A^{\circ p}-B^{\circ p}\right\|_{\infty}\leq p\cdot\beta^{p-1}\cdot\left\|A-B\right\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|A-B\right\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|A-B\right\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is the error of the Chebyshev polynomial method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is the largest entry in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
Bucketing addresses this issue by splitting the entries into groups (buckets) according to their magnitude. In each bucket, the maximum absolute value <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\text{bucket}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mtext>bucket</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\text{bucket}}</annotation></semantics></math> is much smaller than the global maximum <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we can approximate <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(x)</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-B_{\text{bucket}},B_{\text{bucket}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mtext>bucket</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mtext>bucket</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-B_{\text{bucket}},B_{\text{bucket}}]</annotation></semantics></math> using a much lower-degree polynomial, reducing all <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In attention computation, bucketing corresponds to
decomposing the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q,K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q,K)</annotation></semantics></math>-softmax attention matrix into a sum of disjoint components, each of which can be approximated by a polynomial attention. This yields</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)\approx\sum_{\text{buckets }b}p_{b}\left(U_{1}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\left(U_{2}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≈</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mtext>buckets </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)\approx\sum_{\text{buckets }b}p_{b}\left(U_{1}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\left(U_{2}^{\left(\ell,\ell^{\prime}\right)}\right)^{\top}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Each <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial tailored to the value range of its bucket. Because each polynomial is specialized to a narrower range, it
matches the exponential function more closely within that range. Summing these accurate, range-specific approximations produces a result that is closer to the original softmax attention than using a single polynomial approximation over the full range. This concludes our proof sketch for Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5 (Informal version of Theorem ). ‣ Contribution 2—An efficient attention approximation algorithm with no assumption. ‣ 1.3 Our Results ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:173%;">Appendix</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Roadmap.</h5>

<div id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our paper presents two main results. First, we introduce the use of a <em id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single threshold support basis</em> to separate the large and small entries of the query and key matrices <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under the sub-Gaussian assumption, this allows us to design a sub-quadratic time algorithm for approximating the attention computation, without requiring the bounded-entry condition <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|Q\|_{\infty},\|K\|_{\infty}&lt;o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|Q\|_{\infty},\|K\|_{\infty}&lt;o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. We first use Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A PRELIMINARIES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> to present the background of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. Then, we use Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C ATTENTION OPTIMIZATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, and Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D ATTENTION OPTIMIZATION UNDER THE SUB-GAUSSIAN DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> to support our result.</p>
</div>
<div id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, we formally define the support basis and show how to split the large and small entries of the query and key matrices <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> in the attention computation. In Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C ATTENTION OPTIMIZATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, we use our theoretical result on the <em id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single threshold support basis</em> to approximate the attention computation in <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> sparsity time. In Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D ATTENTION OPTIMIZATION UNDER THE SUB-GAUSSIAN DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>, we introduce our sub-Gaussian assumption and show that the number of large entries in <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded, so that with high probability, the running time of our attention approximation algorithm, namely the <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> sparsity time, is sub-quadratic. In Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E GENERALIZATION TO MULTI-LAYER TRANSFORMER ARCHITECTURES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>, we show how we can generalize our algorithm to multi-layer attention.</p>
</div>
<div id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we propose the use of a <em id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multiple thresholds support basis</em> to decompose <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> into the sum of several matrices according to these thresholds. For each resulting component, we apply the polynomial approximation method from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and the sketching technique from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> to approximate and solve all subproblems in sub-quadratic time—without making any distributional assumptions, though at the cost of reduced accuracy. We use Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F MULTIPLE THRESHOLDS SUPPORT BASIS DECOMPOSITION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>, Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G THRESHOLD VALUES SPECIFICATION AND INTERVAL-BASED FACTORIZATION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>, Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H SKETCHING REDUCES THE TIME COMPLEXITY OF POLYNOMIAL ATTENTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>, and Appendix <a href="#A9" title="Appendix I ATTENTION OPTIMIZATION VIA MULTI-THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS AND SKETCHING ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> to support this result.</p>
</div>
<div id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, in Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F MULTIPLE THRESHOLDS SUPPORT BASIS DECOMPOSITION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>, we generalize the theoretical results of the <em id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single threshold support basis</em> to the setting with multiple thresholds and explain how to decompose <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> accordingly. In Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G THRESHOLD VALUES SPECIFICATION AND INTERVAL-BASED FACTORIZATION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>, we specify how to choose the thresholds to ensure that the number of decomposed components remains bounded. In Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H SKETCHING REDUCES THE TIME COMPLEXITY OF POLYNOMIAL ATTENTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>, we adapt the sketching techniques from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> to our setting. In Appendix <a href="#A9" title="Appendix I ATTENTION OPTIMIZATION VIA MULTI-THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS AND SKETCHING ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>, we combine the sketching techniques with our <em id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multiple thresholds support basis</em> to approximate the attention computation in sub-quadratic time, without assuming bounded entries or any distributional conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, in Appendix <a href="#A10" title="Appendix J MORE FACTS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>, we present the basic mathematical facts used throughout the paper to support our proofs. In Appendix <a href="#A11" title="Appendix K EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">K</span></a>, we present our experimental results. In Appendix <a href="#A12" title="Appendix L MORE RELATED WORK ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">L</span></a>, we present additional related works. In Appendix <a href="#A13" title="Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M</span></a>, we show the entry distributions of the query and key matrices on different transformer architectures across different layers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notation.</h5>

<div id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any positive integer <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,d</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R},\mathbb{R}^{n},\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R},\mathbb{R}^{n},\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> as sets containing all real numbers, all <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vectors, and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times d</annotation></semantics></math> matrices, whose entries are all in <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]:=\{1,2,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]:=\{1,2,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any set <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X|</annotation></semantics></math> to denote its cardinality, namely the number of elements in this set. For all sets <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X\times Y:=\{\left(x,y\right)\mid x\in X,y\in Y\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times Y:=\{\left(x,y\right)\mid x\in X,y\in Y\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For all <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor a\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor a\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> as the largest integer satisfying <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor a\rfloor\leq a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor a\rfloor\leq a</annotation></semantics></math>. For all <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, their inner product is <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle x,y\rangle:=\sum_{i=1}^{d}x_{i}y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle x,y\rangle:=\sum_{i=1}^{d}x_{i}y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. With any arbitrary <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math>, the Hadamard product <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circ</annotation></semantics></math> is a binary operation: <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\circ y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\circ y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x\circ y)_{i}:=x_{i}\cdot y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x\circ y)_{i}:=x_{i}\cdot y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. For all <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> being a positive integer or <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> norm of <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|_{p}:=\left(\sum_{i\in[d]}|x_{i}|^{p}\right)^{1/p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|_{p}:=\left(\sum_{i\in[d]}|x_{i}|^{p}\right)^{1/p}</annotation></semantics></math>. We let <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf 1}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf 1}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf 0}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf 0}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively denote the <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vectors whose entries are all <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>’s and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>’s. Similarly, we respectively define <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf 1}_{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf 1}_{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf 0}_{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf 0}_{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times d</annotation></semantics></math> matrix whose entries are all <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>’s and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>’s. We define <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diag(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diag(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the diagonal matrix with <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diag(x)_{i,i}=x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diag(x)_{i,i}=x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We let <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i,j}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i,j}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>-th entry of <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i,*}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i,*}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th row, and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{*,j}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{*,j}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th column. We let <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the transpose. <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A\|_{F},\|A\|_{\infty}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A\|_{F},\|A\|_{\infty}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively are the Frobenius norm and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> norm, where <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A\|_{F}:=\sqrt{\sum_{i\in[n]}\sum_{j\in[d]}|A_{i,j}|^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A\|_{F}:=\sqrt{\sum_{i\in[n]}\sum_{j\in[d]}|A_{i,j}|^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A\|_{\infty}:=\max_{i\in[n],j\in[d]}|A_{i,j}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A\|_{\infty}:=\max_{i\in[n],j\in[d]}|A_{i,j}|</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\supp(A):=\{(i,j)\in[n]\times[d]\mid A_{i,j}\neq 0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\supp(A):=\{(i,j)\in[n]\times[d]\mid A_{i,j}\neq 0\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1},n_{2},d_{1},d_{2},p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1},n_{2},d_{1},d_{2},p</annotation></semantics></math> be positive integers. We define <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(A)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(A)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\circ p}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\circ p}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> as the entry-wise exponential and power, namely for all <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(A)_{i,j}:=\exp\left(A_{i,j}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(A)_{i,j}:=\exp\left(A_{i,j}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(A^{\circ p}\right)_{i,j}:=\left(A_{i,j}\right)^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(A^{\circ p}\right)_{i,j}:=\left(A_{i,j}\right)^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A\|_{p}:=\sqrt[p]{\sum_{i,j}|A_{i,j}|^{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mroot><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mi>p</mi></mroot></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A\|_{p}:=\sqrt[p]{\sum_{i,j}|A_{i,j}|^{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{1}\times d_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{1}\times d_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{2}\times d_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{2}\times d_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the Kronecker product between matrices (or vectors if <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\otimes B\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{1}n_{2}\times d_{1}d_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\otimes B\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{1}n_{2}\times d_{1}d_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A\otimes B)_{(i_{1}-1)n_{2}+i_{2},(j_{1}-1)d_{2}+j_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A\otimes B)_{(i_{1}-1)n_{2}+i_{2},(j_{1}-1)d_{2}+j_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i_{1},j_{1}}B_{i_{2},j_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i_{1},j_{1}}B_{i_{2},j_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}\in[n_{1}],j_{1}\in[d_{1}],i_{2}\in[n_{2}],j_{2}\in[d_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}\in[n_{1}],j_{1}\in[d_{1}],i_{2}\in[n_{2}],j_{2}\in[d_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>. We let <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\otimes p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\otimes p}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p3.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underbrace{A\otimes A\otimes\cdots\otimes A}_{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{1}^{p}\times d_{1}^{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mi>p</mi></munder><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underbrace{A\otimes A\otimes\cdots\otimes A}_{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{1}^{p}\times d_{1}^{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For a differentiable function <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote its derivative.
For a probability space <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\Pr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Pr</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\Pr)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> is the sample space, <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra of events, and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr:\mathcal{F}\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr:\mathcal{F}\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is the probability measure, we define the discrete random variable <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> so that the expectation of <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\E[X]:=\sum_{i}x_{i}\cdot\Pr[X=x_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝔼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\E[X]:=\sum_{i}x_{i}\cdot\Pr[X=x_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>, and for all <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the indicator function <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}[A]:\Omega\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}[A]:\Omega\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}[A](\omega):=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}[A](\omega):=1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}[A](\omega):=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}[A](\omega):=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\notin A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\notin A</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>PRELIMINARIES</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide the theoretical foundation necessary to understand and contextualize our contributions in attention approximation. We give a summary of the prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, which established sub-quadratic time algorithms for attention approximation under strong boundedness assumptions. We also present the important mathematical properties from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Background: Technique Overview of the Polynomial Method ‣ Appendix A PRELIMINARIES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>, we give an overview of techniques in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. In Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Background: Sketching and Polynomial Attention ‣ Appendix A PRELIMINARIES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>, we present the background related to the sketching technique and polynomial attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Background: Technique Overview of the Polynomial Method</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since the <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(Q,K\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(Q,K\right)</annotation></semantics></math>-softmax-attention matrix is defined as <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> approximate the exponential function <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with a degree-<math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> Chebyshev polynomial <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. As both <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>exp</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> are applied entry-wise, for each <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, we can express <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="A1.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)_{i,j}=p\left(\langle Q_{i,*},K_{j,*}\rangle/d\right)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)_{i,j}=p\left(\langle Q_{i,*},K_{j,*}\rangle/d\right)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~p\left(\frac{1}{d}\sum_{k=1}^{d}Q_{i,k}K_{j,k}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~p\left(\frac{1}{d}\sum_{k=1}^{d}Q_{i,k}K_{j,k}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">After expanding this polynomial, it has at most <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\binom{2d+2g}{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\binom{2d+2g}{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> number of monomials <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Additionally, for all <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in[r]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in[r]</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\alpha},K_{\alpha}\subseteq[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\alpha},K_{\alpha}\subseteq[d]</annotation></semantics></math>, such that for all <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\alpha}\in Q_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\alpha}\in Q_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\alpha}\in K_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\alpha}\in K_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, the monomial can be expressed as</p>
<table id="A1.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C_{\alpha}\prod_{q_{\alpha}\in Q_{\alpha}}Q_{i,q_{\alpha}}^{\gamma_{Q}\left(i,q_{\alpha}\right)}\prod_{k_{\alpha}\in K_{\alpha}}K_{j,k_{\alpha}}^{\gamma_{K}\left(j,k_{\alpha}\right)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C_{\alpha}\prod_{q_{\alpha}\in Q_{\alpha}}Q_{i,q_{\alpha}}^{\gamma_{Q}\left(i,q_{\alpha}\right)}\prod_{k_{\alpha}\in K_{\alpha}}K_{j,k_{\alpha}}^{\gamma_{K}\left(j,k_{\alpha}\right)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\alpha}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\alpha}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{Q}:[n]\times Q_{\alpha}\to\Z_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em">ℤ</mo><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{Q}:[n]\times Q_{\alpha}\to\Z_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{K}:[n]\times K_{\alpha}\to\Z_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em">ℤ</mo><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{K}:[n]\times K_{\alpha}\to\Z_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. For all <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, we respectively define each row vectors of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(U_{1}\right)_{i,*},\left(U_{2}\right)_{j,*}\in\mathbb{R}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(U_{1}\right)_{i,*},\left(U_{2}\right)_{j,*}\in\mathbb{R}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, by <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(U_{1}\right)_{i,\alpha}:=C_{\alpha}\prod_{q_{\alpha}\in Q_{\alpha}}Q_{i,q_{\alpha}}^{\gamma_{Q}\left(i,q_{\alpha}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(U_{1}\right)_{i,\alpha}:=C_{\alpha}\prod_{q_{\alpha}\in Q_{\alpha}}Q_{i,q_{\alpha}}^{\gamma_{Q}\left(i,q_{\alpha}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(U_{2}\right)_{j,\alpha}:=\prod_{k_{\alpha}\in K_{\alpha}}K_{j,k_{\alpha}}^{\gamma_{K}\left(j,k_{\alpha}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(U_{2}\right)_{j,\alpha}:=\prod_{k_{\alpha}\in K_{\alpha}}K_{j,k_{\alpha}}^{\gamma_{K}\left(j,k_{\alpha}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we further simplify Eq. (<a href="#A1.E5" title="In A.1 Background: Technique Overview of the Polynomial Method ‣ Appendix A PRELIMINARIES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) as:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)_{i,j}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)_{i,j}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\sum_{\alpha\in[r]}C_{\alpha}\prod_{q_{\alpha}\in Q_{\alpha}}Q_{i,q_{\alpha}}^{\gamma_{Q}\left(i,q_{\alpha}\right)}\prod_{k_{\alpha}\in K_{\alpha}}K_{j,k_{\alpha}}^{\gamma_{K}\left(j,k_{\alpha}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\sum_{\alpha\in[r]}C_{\alpha}\prod_{q_{\alpha}\in Q_{\alpha}}Q_{i,q_{\alpha}}^{\gamma_{Q}\left(i,q_{\alpha}\right)}\prod_{k_{\alpha}\in K_{\alpha}}K_{j,k_{\alpha}}^{\gamma_{K}\left(j,k_{\alpha}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\left\langle\left(U_{1}\right)_{i,*},\left(U_{2}\right)_{j,*}\right\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\left\langle\left(U_{1}\right)_{i,*},\left(U_{2}\right)_{j,*}\right\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\left(U_{1}\right)_{i,*}^{\top}\left(U_{2}\right)_{j,*}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\left(U_{1}\right)_{i,*}^{\top}\left(U_{2}\right)_{j,*}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we focus on time complexity of computing this quantity.
We know that when expanding <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\left(\frac{1}{d}\sum_{k=1}^{d}Q_{i,k}K_{j,k}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\left(\frac{1}{d}\sum_{k=1}^{d}Q_{i,k}K_{j,k}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, each monomial in this degree-<math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> Chebyshev polynomial must satisfy <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{q_{\alpha}\in Q_{\alpha}}\gamma_{Q}\left(i,q_{\alpha}\right),\sum_{k_{\alpha}\in K_{\alpha}}\gamma_{K}\left(j,k_{\alpha}\right)\leq g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{q_{\alpha}\in Q_{\alpha}}\gamma_{Q}\left(i,q_{\alpha}\right),\sum_{k_{\alpha}\in K_{\alpha}}\gamma_{K}\left(j,k_{\alpha}\right)\leq g</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, each monomial (Eq. (<a href="#A1.E6" title="In A.1 Background: Technique Overview of the Polynomial Method ‣ Appendix A PRELIMINARIES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>)) requires <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(g\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(g\right)</annotation></semantics></math> time to construct. Since each Chebyshev polynomial contains <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\binom{2d+2g}{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\binom{2d+2g}{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> monomials, and we need to construct such a Chebyshev polynomial for each row vector <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(U_{1}\right)_{i,*},\left(U_{2}\right)_{j,*}\in\mathbb{R}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(U_{1}\right)_{i,*},\left(U_{2}\right)_{j,*}\in\mathbb{R}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the full matrices <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the total time complexity is <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(nrg\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(nrg\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, to ensure that the Chebyshev polynomial is a good approximation—i.e., <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|p\left(x\right)-\exp\left(x\right)|&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|p\left(x\right)-\exp\left(x\right)|&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in[0,B]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in[0,B]</annotation></semantics></math>—<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> shows that the degree <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> must be</p>
<table id="A1.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g=\Theta\left(\max\left\{\frac{\log\left(1/\epsilon\right)}{\log\left(\log\left(1/\epsilon\right)/B\right)},B\right\}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g=\Theta\left(\max\left\{\frac{\log\left(1/\epsilon\right)}{\log\left(\log\left(1/\epsilon\right)/B\right)},B\right\}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This is where the bounded entry assumption originates. To ensure this polynomial method can have a fast runtime <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,r\leq n^{o\left(1\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,r\leq n^{o\left(1\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> (so that <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(nrg\right)=O\left(n^{1+o\left(1\right)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(nrg\right)=O\left(n^{1+o\left(1\right)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>) to construct <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> and a small error <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|A-U_{1}U_{2}^{\top}\right\|_{\infty}&lt;\epsilon=1/\poly\left(n\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|A-U_{1}U_{2}^{\top}\right\|_{\infty}&lt;\epsilon=1/\poly\left(n\right)</annotation></semantics></math>—<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> shows that we must restrict <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=O\left(\log n\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=O\left(\log n\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, applying <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> to construct <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}=\diag\left(\underbrace{U_{1}}_{n\times r}\left(\underbrace{U_{2}^{\top}}_{r\times n}\underbrace{{\bf 1}_{n}}_{n\times 1}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}=\diag\left(\underbrace{U_{1}}_{n\times r}\left(\underbrace{U_{2}^{\top}}_{r\times n}\underbrace{{\bf 1}_{n}}_{n\times 1}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and in attention computation <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\underbrace{\widetilde{D}}_{n\times n}\underbrace{U_{1}}_{n\times r}\right)\left(\underbrace{U_{2}^{\top}}_{r\times n}\underbrace{V}_{n\times d}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo><mo>~</mo></munderover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\underbrace{\widetilde{D}}_{n\times n}\underbrace{U_{1}}_{n\times r}\right)\left(\underbrace{U_{2}^{\top}}_{r\times n}\underbrace{V}_{n\times d}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> also require nearly linear time <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(nrd\right)=O\left(n^{1+o\left(1\right)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(nrd\right)=O\left(n^{1+o\left(1\right)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}=\diag\left(U_{1}U_{2}^{\top}{\bf 1}_{n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}=\diag\left(U_{1}U_{2}^{\top}{\bf 1}_{n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is a diagonal matrix.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1.SSS1" title="A.1.1 Background: Low Rank Approximation ‣ A.1 Background: Technique Overview of the Polynomial Method ‣ Appendix A PRELIMINARIES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1.1</span></a>, we introduce the notion of <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,r)</annotation></semantics></math>-approximations via low-rank matrix factorizations and present its connection to approximating matrix exponentials. In Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1.SSS2" title="A.1.2 Background: Approximating the Attention Computation Assuming 𝑑=𝑂(log𝑛) and Bounded Entries ‣ A.1 Background: Technique Overview of the Polynomial Method ‣ Appendix A PRELIMINARIES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1.2</span></a>, we present the algorithm that when <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=O\left(\log n\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=O\left(\log n\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and entries of <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K,V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K,V</annotation></semantics></math> are bounded by <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o(\sqrt{\log n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o(\sqrt{\log n})</annotation></semantics></math>, it outputs an approximation to the attention computation in <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{1+o(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{1+o(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> time. In Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1.SSS3" title="A.1.3 Background: Main Results of [] ‣ A.1 Background: Technique Overview of the Polynomial Method ‣ Appendix A PRELIMINARIES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1.3</span></a>, we formally state the main results from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> the lower and upper bounds for attention approximation.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.1.1 </span>Background: Low Rank Approximation</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we review concepts of matrix low-rank approximation. A key idea is to approximate the softmax attention matrix, which is dense and expensive to compute, using a low-rank decomposition constructed from polynomial expansions. This technique is central to the method proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, where the exponential function is approximated by a Chebyshev polynomial, and the resulting polynomial matrix is factorized into a product of two low-rank matrices. This allows for efficient computation of the attention matrix without explicitly forming it. We formally define the notion of an <math id="A1.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,r)</annotation></semantics></math>-approximation and present a key lemma from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> that shows how to construct such a factorization efficiently.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.1</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> denote a positive integer. Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in\left(0,0.1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in\left(0,0.1\right)</annotation></semantics></math> denote an accuracy parameter.
Given a matrix <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we say <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\epsilon,r\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\epsilon,r\right)</annotation></semantics></math>-approximation of <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> if</span></p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{A}=U_{1}\cdot U_{2}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{A}=U_{1}\cdot U_{2}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for some matrices </span><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (i.e., </span><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{A}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has rank at most </span><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">), and</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\widetilde{A}_{i,j}-A_{i,j}|\leq\epsilon\cdot A_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\widetilde{A}_{i,j}-A_{i,j}|\leq\epsilon\cdot A_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(i,j\right)\in[n]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(i,j\right)\in[n]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.2</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lemma 3.2 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=XY^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=XY^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a matrix with
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\left(x\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\left(x\right)</annotation></semantics></math> denote a degree-<math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> polynomial, and define <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\binom{2\left(g+d\right)}{2g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\binom{2\left(g+d\right)}{2g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is an algorithm that runs in <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(nrg\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(nrg\right)</annotation></semantics></math> time and, given as input the matrix <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y</annotation></semantics></math>, constructs matrices <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\left(M\right)=U_{1}U_{2}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\left(M\right)=U_{1}U_{2}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. (Here, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\left(M\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\left(M\right)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the entry-wise application of <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.)</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.1.2 </span>Background: Approximating the Attention Computation Assuming <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=O\left(\log n\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=O\left(\log n\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and Bounded Entries</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe the algorithm introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> for approximating the attention computation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> It takes <math id="alg2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d},K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d},V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d},K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d},V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> as input and approximates the attention computation (Definition <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 1.3 (The approximate attention computation). ‣ 1.2 Background ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>) under the assumptions <math id="alg2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=O\left(\log n\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=O\left(\log n\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|Q\right\|_{\infty}\leq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|Q\right\|_{\infty}\leq B</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|K\right\|_{\infty}\leq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|K\right\|_{\infty}\leq B</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="alg2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|V\right\|_{\infty}\leq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|V\right\|_{\infty}\leq B</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="alg2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
