<div id="A1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.3</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Corollary 2.2 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in\left(0,0.1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in\left(0,0.1\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
There is a polynomial <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:=\Theta\left(\max\left\{\frac{\log\left(1/\epsilon\right)}{\log\left(\log\left(1/\epsilon\right)/B\right)},B\right\}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:=\Theta\left(\max\left\{\frac{\log\left(1/\epsilon\right)}{\log\left(\log\left(1/\epsilon\right)/B\right)},B\right\}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in[-B,B]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in[-B,B]</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|P\left(x\right)-\exp\left(x\right)|&lt;\epsilon\cdot\exp\left(x\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|P\left(x\right)-\exp\left(x\right)|&lt;\epsilon\cdot\exp\left(x\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.4</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(An improved version of Lemma 3.4 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|QK^{\top}\right\|_{\infty}\leq dB^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|QK^{\top}\right\|_{\infty}\leq dB^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:=\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:=\exp\left(QK^{\top}/d\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>. For accuracy parameter <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in\left(0,1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in\left(0,1\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a positive integer <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> bounded above by</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g=O\left(\max\left\{\frac{\log\left(1/\epsilon\right)}{\log\left(\log\left(1/\epsilon\right)/B\right)},B^{2}\right\}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g=O\left(\max\left\{\frac{\log\left(1/\epsilon\right)}{\log\left(\log\left(1/\epsilon\right)/B\right)},B^{2}\right\}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and a positive integer <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> bounded above by</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r\leq\binom{2\left(g+d\right)}{2g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r\leq\binom{2\left(g+d\right)}{2g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">such that:
There is a matrix <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> that is an <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\epsilon,r\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\epsilon,r\right)</annotation></semantics></math>-approximation (Definition <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition A.1. ‣ A.1.1 Background: Low Rank Approximation ‣ A.1 Background: Technique Overview of the Polynomial Method ‣ Appendix A PRELIMINARIES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>) of <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, the matrices <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> defining <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{A}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed in <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(n\cdot r\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(n\cdot r\right)</annotation></semantics></math> time.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the assumption that <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|QK^{\top}\right\|_{\infty}\leq dB^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|QK^{\top}\right\|_{\infty}\leq dB^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can get</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|QK^{\top}/d\right\|_{\infty}\leq B^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|QK^{\top}/d\right\|_{\infty}\leq B^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, applying Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma A.3 (Corollary 2.2 in []). ‣ A.1.2 Background: Approximating the Attention Computation Assuming 𝑑=𝑂(log𝑛) and Bounded Entries ‣ A.1 Background: Technique Overview of the Polynomial Method ‣ Appendix A PRELIMINARIES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> (with bound <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on its entries), there is a degree-<math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> polynomial <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> such that the matrix <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{A}=P\left(M\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{A}=P\left(M\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\epsilon,r\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\epsilon,r\right)</annotation></semantics></math>-approximation to <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. We can then compute <math id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> using Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma A.2 (Lemma 3.2 in []). ‣ A.1.1 Background: Low Rank Approximation ‣ A.1 Background: Technique Overview of the Polynomial Method ‣ Appendix A PRELIMINARIES ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>, which gives the bound</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r=\binom{2\left(g+d\right)}{2g}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r=\binom{2\left(g+d\right)}{2g}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.SSS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">This completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.1.3 </span>Background: Main Results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite></h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we formally present the core theoretical results from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, which establish both upper and lower bounds for the approximate attention computation problem.
The upper bound shows that when the input matrices <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K,V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K,V\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> have entries bounded in <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> norm by <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=o(\sqrt{\log n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=o(\sqrt{\log n})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the hidden dimension satisfies <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=O(\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=O(\log n)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the attention computation can be approximated to within error <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=1/\mathrm{poly}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=1/\mathrm{poly}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> in nearly linear time <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{1+o(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{1+o(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A.5</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Upper bound, Theorem 3.8 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With <math id="A1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=O(\log n),B=o(\sqrt{\log n}),\epsilon_{a}=1/\poly(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">𝑂𝑃𝐸𝑁</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=O(\log n),B=o(\sqrt{\log n}),\epsilon_{a}=1/\poly(n))</annotation></semantics></math>, the approximate attention computation problem (see Definition <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 1.3 (The approximate attention computation). ‣ 1.2 Background ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>) can be solved in time <math id="A1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal T}_{\mathrm{mat}}(n,n^{o(1)},d)=n^{1+o(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">𝒯</mi><mi>mat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal T}_{\mathrm{mat}}(n,n^{o(1)},d)=n^{1+o(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, the lower bound establishes a fundamental computational limitation: under the SETH, any algorithm that approximates attention within accuracy <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=1/\mathrm{poly}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=1/\mathrm{poly}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> must take at least <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{2-o(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{2-o(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> time when <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\Theta(\sqrt{\log n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\Theta(\sqrt{\log n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.6</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (<span id="A1.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">SETH</span>), Hypothesis 4.1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="A1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there is a positive integer <math id="A1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="A1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{SAT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖲𝖠𝖳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{SAT}</annotation></semantics></math> on formulas with <math id="A1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> variables cannot be solved in <math id="A1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(2^{(1-\epsilon)n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(2^{(1-\epsilon)n})</annotation></semantics></math> time, even by a randomized algorithm.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A.7</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lower bound, Theorem 4.6 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assuming </span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">SETH</span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for every sufficiently small <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there are constants
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\alpha}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\alpha}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\beta}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\beta}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> such that Approximate Attention Computation (Definition <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 1.3 (The approximate attention computation). ‣ 1.2 Background ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>) for parameters <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=O(\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=O(\log n)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=C_{\beta}\sqrt{\log n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=C_{\beta}\sqrt{\log n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{a}=n^{-C_{\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{a}=n^{-C_{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math> requires <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(n^{2-q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(n^{2-q})</annotation></semantics></math> time.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Background: Sketching and Polynomial Attention</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Below, we define a version of polynomial attention from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.8</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Polynomial Attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be defined as <math id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(z)=z^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(z)=z^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(W)_{i,j}=g(W_{i,j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(W)_{i,j}=g(W_{i,j})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is a matrix and <math id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)_{i}=g(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)_{i}=g(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector. Given the input sequence <math id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and the query, key, and value weights <math id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q},W_{K},W_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q},W_{K},W_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the polynomial attention computation is defined as:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D^{-1}AV," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D^{-1}AV,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=g(XW_{Q}W_{K}^{\top}X^{\top}/\sqrt{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=g(XW_{Q}W_{K}^{\top}X^{\top}/\sqrt{d})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D:=\diag(A{\bf 1}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D:=\diag(A{\bf 1}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As demonstrated in Theorem 1.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, one can construct a randomized feature map <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for the degree-<math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> polynomial kernel that ensures the resulting approximate attention weights remain non-negative, satisfy provable error bounds, and can be computed in time proportional to the sequence length <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A.9</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Theorem 1.1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> be an even integer, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in\left(0,0.5\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in\left(0,0.5\right)</annotation></semantics></math> be an error parameter. Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> be the dimension of the vectors to be mapped. There exists a randomized feature mapping</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{z^{2}},\quad\text{for }z=\Theta\left(\frac{p}{\epsilon^{2}}\log\frac{1}{\delta}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{z^{2}},\quad\text{for }z=\Theta\left(\frac{p}{\epsilon^{2}}\log\frac{1}{\delta}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">defined as <math id="A1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}(x):=\left(Sx^{\otimes\left(p/2\right)}\right)^{\otimes 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}(x):=\left(Sx^{\otimes\left(p/2\right)}\right)^{\otimes 2}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in\mathbb{R}^{z\times d^{p/2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in\mathbb{R}^{z\times d^{p/2}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sketching matrix, such that for any set of vectors <math id="A1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{q_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\right\}_{i\in[n]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{q_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\right\}_{i\in[n]}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{k_{j}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\right\}_{j\in[n]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{k_{j}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\right\}_{j\in[n]}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following hold with probability at least <math id="A1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ol id="A1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\langle\phi^{\prime}\left(q_{i}\right),\phi^{\prime}\left(k_{j}\right)\right\rangle\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\langle\phi^{\prime}\left(q_{i}\right),\phi^{\prime}\left(k_{j}\right)\right\rangle\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A1.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i,j}|\left\langle\phi^{\prime}\left(q_{i}\right),\phi^{\prime}\left(k_{j}\right)\right\rangle-\left\langle q_{i},k_{j}\right\rangle^{p}|^{2}\leq\epsilon^{2}\sum_{i,j}\left\|q_{i}\right\|_{2}^{2p}\left\|k_{j}\right\|_{2}^{2p};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i,j}|\left\langle\phi^{\prime}\left(q_{i}\right),\phi^{\prime}\left(k_{j}\right)\right\rangle-\left\langle q_{i},k_{j}\right\rangle^{p}|^{2}\leq\epsilon^{2}\sum_{i,j}\left\|q_{i}\right\|_{2}^{2p}\left\|k_{j}\right\|_{2}^{2p};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Computing </span><math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}\left(x\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}\left(x\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> requires:</span></p>
<ul id="A1.I2.i3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{p}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{p}{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> matrix-vector multiplications with matrices of size </span><math id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{p}{2}-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{p}{2}-2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> matrix-vector multiplications with matrices of size </span><math id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\times z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\times z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{p}{2}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{p}{2}-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Hadamard products of </span><math id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-dimensional vectors,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and </span><math id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> self-Kronecker product of an </span><math id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I2.i3.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-dimensional vector.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze the concentration and tail behavior of random variables—particularly in the context of sketching and randomized linear algebra—it is useful to work with their moment norms. The following definition introduces the <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> norm of a real-valued random variable, which captures its <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th moment in a normalized form. These norms play a key role in bounding approximation errors and analyzing stability under random projections. An important property of <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> norms is that they satisfy the triangle inequality, as guaranteed by the Minkowski inequality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.10</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Definition 4.1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every integer <math id="A1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and any random variable <math id="A1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|X\|_{L_{t}}=\left(\mathbb{E}\left[|X|^{t}\right]\right)^{1/t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|X\|_{L_{t}}=\left(\mathbb{E}\left[|X|^{t}\right]\right)^{1/t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Note that <math id="A1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|X+Y\|_{L_{t}}\leq\|X\|_{L_{t}}+\|Y\|_{L_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|X+Y\|_{L_{t}}\leq\|X\|_{L_{t}}+\|Y\|_{L_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> for any random variables <math id="A1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y</annotation></semantics></math> by the Minkowski Inequality.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Below, we present the JL moment property: it provides a probabilistic guarantee on how well a random matrix preserves the norm of any fixed unit vector—not just with high probability, but in expectation and in <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> norm. This property strengthens the classical JL lemma by quantifying how tightly concentrated the squared norm <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|Sx\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|Sx\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is around its mean.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.11</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(JL Moment Property, Definition 4.2 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every positive integer <math id="A1.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and every <math id="A1.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta,\epsilon\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta,\epsilon\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we say a distribution over random matrices <math id="A1.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> has the <math id="A1.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta,t)</annotation></semantics></math>-JL-moment property, when</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\|Sx\|_{2}^{2}-1\right\|_{L_{t}}\leq\epsilon\delta^{1/t}\quad\text{and}\quad\mathbb{E}\left[\|Sx\|_{2}^{2}\right]=1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\|Sx\|_{2}^{2}-1\right\|_{L_{t}}\leq\epsilon\delta^{1/t}\quad\text{and}\quad\mathbb{E}\left[\|Sx\|_{2}^{2}\right]=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem11.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="A1.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, the sketching matrix satisfying the JL Moment Property has the following property:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.12</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Two vector JL Moment Property, Lemma 4.1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="A1.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="A1.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> has the <math id="A1.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta,t)</annotation></semantics></math>-JL Moment Property, then</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|(Sx)^{\top}(Sy)-x^{\top}y\right\|_{L_{t}}\leq\epsilon\delta^{1/t}\|x\|_{2}\|y\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|(Sx)^{\top}(Sy)-x^{\top}y\right\|_{L_{t}}\leq\epsilon\delta^{1/t}\|x\|_{2}\|y\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>(BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Disjoint Matrices and Their Properties ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>, we present the definition of disjoint matrices and analyze their mathematical properties. In Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Support Basis ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>, we give the formal definition of support basis and construct a single threshold support basis for <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. In Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Bounded Entry ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>, we generalize the bounded entry lemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> from the threshold <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=o\left(\sqrt{\log n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> to any arbitrary threshold being a positive real number. In Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Running Time and Sparsity Analysis ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>, we show that if the number of large entries in <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is small, then we can compute certain matrix multiplications in <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> sparsity time. In Appendix <a href="#A2.SS5" title="B.5 (Batch) Gaussian Kernel Density Estimation ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>, we use our constructed single threshold support basis to design an <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> sparsity time algorithm to approximate the (batch) Gaussian kernel density estimation <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AV</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Disjoint Matrices and Their Properties</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to decompose the <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(Q,K\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(Q,K\right)</annotation></semantics></math>-softmax-attention matrix into simpler components, we begin by introducing the notion of disjoint matrices. This disjointness condition ensures that matrix components do not overlap in their non-zero entries, which is a crucial property that enables additive decompositions of matrix exponentials. We formalize this idea in the following definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition B.1</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Disjoint matrices)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{A_{k}\right\}_{k\in[l]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{A_{k}\right\}_{k\in[l]}</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> matrices, where <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> is an arbitrary positive integer. We say <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>’s are disjoint matrices if for all <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in[l]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in[l]</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{k}\in[l]\setminus\left\{k\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{k}\in[l]\setminus\left\{k\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(A_{k}\right)_{i,j}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(A_{k}\right)_{i,j}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(A_{\overline{k}}\right)_{i,j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(A_{\overline{k}}\right)_{i,j}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we show how the exponential of a sum of disjoint matrices can be simplified. Since disjoint matrices do not share any overlapping non-zero entries, the exponential of their sum equals the sum of their exponentials minus a constant matrix.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_fact">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fact B.2</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B,C\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B,C\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> be disjoint matrices (see Definition <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition B.1 (Disjoint matrices). ‣ B.1 Disjoint Matrices and Their Properties ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, we have</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\exp\left(B+C\right)=\exp\left(B\right)+\exp\left(C\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\exp\left(B+C\right)=\exp\left(B\right)+\exp\left(C\right)-{\bf 1}_{n\times n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Without loss of generality, suppose <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i,j}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i,j}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i,j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i,j}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for all <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\exp\left(B+C\right)_{i,j}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\exp\left(B+C\right)_{i,j}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\exp\left(B_{i,j}+C_{i,j}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\exp\left(B_{i,j}+C_{i,j}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\exp\left(C\right)_{i,j}+1-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\exp\left(C\right)_{i,j}+1-1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\exp\left(C\right)_{i,j}+\exp\left(B\right)_{i,j}-\left({\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)_{i,j}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\exp\left(C\right)_{i,j}+\exp\left(B\right)_{i,j}-\left({\bf 1}_{n\times n}\right)_{i,j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the first step follows from the fact that <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>exp</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp</annotation></semantics></math> is applied entry-wisely, the second step follows from <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i,j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i,j}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the last step follows from <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp\left(0\right)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp\left(0\right)=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Support Basis</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To construct a support basis for the matrix <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, we must first define how to partition the matrix into “large” and “small” components based on the entries in <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition B.3</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> denote a threshold, <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> denote a fixed constant.
Let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the query and key matrices, respectively. We decompose these matrices as <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=Q^{\left(L\right)}+Q^{\left(s\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=Q^{\left(L\right)}+Q^{\left(s\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=K^{\left(L\right)}+K^{\left(s\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=K^{\left(L\right)}+K^{\left(s\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, where:</span></p>
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\mathrm{supp}\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\right)\right|,\left|\mathrm{supp}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)\right|\leq Cn^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>supp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\mathrm{supp}\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\right)\right|,\left|\mathrm{supp}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)\right|\leq Cn^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and for all </span><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, if </span><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|Q_{i,j}|&gt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|Q_{i,j}|&gt;T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(L\right)}_{i,j}:=Q_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(L\right)}_{i,j}:=Q_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">; otherwise, </span><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(L\right)}_{i,j}:=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(L\right)}_{i,j}:=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. We define </span><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in a similar manner.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all </span><math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, if </span><math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|Q_{i,j}|\leq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|Q_{i,j}|\leq T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(s\right)}_{i,j}:=Q_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(s\right)}_{i,j}:=Q_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">; otherwise, </span><math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(s\right)}_{i,j}:=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(s\right)}_{i,j}:=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. We define </span><math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\left(s\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\left(s\right)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in a similar manner.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(L)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(s)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(s)}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition B.4</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the query and the key matrices <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(L\right)},Q^{\left(s\right)},K^{\left(L\right)},K^{\left(s\right)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(L\right)},Q^{\left(s\right)},K^{\left(L\right)},K^{\left(s\right)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> be defined as in Definition <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition B.3. ‣ B.2 Support Basis ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>. Let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define matrices <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(s\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(s\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A^{\left(L\right)}_{i,j}:=\begin{cases}\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{i,j}&amp;\quad\quad\mathrm{if}~\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}\not=0\\
0&amp;\quad\quad\mathrm{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>if</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>otherwise</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A^{\left(L\right)}_{i,j}:=\begin{cases}\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{i,j}&amp;\quad\quad\mathrm{if}~\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}\not=0\\
0&amp;\quad\quad\mathrm{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A2.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A^{\left(s\right)}_{i,j}:=\begin{cases}0&amp;\quad\quad\mathrm{if}~\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}\not=0\\
\left(Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}&amp;\quad\quad\mathrm{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>if</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>otherwise</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A^{\left(s\right)}_{i,j}:=\begin{cases}0&amp;\quad\quad\mathrm{if}~\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}\not=0\\
\left(Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}&amp;\quad\quad\mathrm{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we provide the formal definition of the support basis. For a given matrix <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, it requires all matrices in this basis to be disjoint (see Definition <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition B.1 (Disjoint matrices). ‣ B.1 Disjoint Matrices and Their Properties ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>) to ensure the additive decomposition (see Fact <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem2" title="Fact B.2. ‣ B.1 Disjoint Matrices and Their Properties ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>) and the sum of all matrices in this basis to be equal to the given matrix <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition B.5</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Support basis)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{A_{k}\right\}_{k\in[l]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{A_{k}\right\}_{k\in[l]}</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> matrices, where <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> is an arbitrary positive integer.
Given a matrix <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{A_{k}\right\}_{k\in[l]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{A_{k}\right\}_{k\in[l]}</annotation></semantics></math> is a support basis of <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> if</span></p>
<ol id="A2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k\in[l]}A_{k}=A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k\in[l]}A_{k}=A</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">’s are disjoint matrices (see Definition </span><a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition B.1 (Disjoint matrices). ‣ B.1 Disjoint Matrices and Their Properties ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a><span id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Below, we formally prove that the resulting matrices <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(s)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(s)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(L)}</annotation></semantics></math> (as defined in Definition <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem4" title="Definition B.4. ‣ B.2 Support Basis ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>) are disjoint and together sum to <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, forming a valid support basis of <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.6</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the query and the key matrices <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,K\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\left(L\right)},Q^{\left(s\right)},K^{\left(L\right)},K^{\left(s\right)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\left(L\right)},Q^{\left(s\right)},K^{\left(L\right)},K^{\left(s\right)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> be defined as in Definition <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition B.3. ‣ B.2 Support Basis ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a> and <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)},A^{\left(s\right)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)},A^{\left(s\right)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> be defined as in Definition <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem4" title="Definition B.4. ‣ B.2 Support Basis ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{A^{\left(L\right)},A^{\left(s\right)}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{A^{\left(L\right)},A^{\left(s\right)}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a support basis of the matrix <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first show the first condition of the support basis, namely <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(s\right)}+A^{\left(L\right)}=QK^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(s\right)}+A^{\left(L\right)}=QK^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">By definition <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem4" title="Definition B.4. ‣ B.2 Support Basis ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>, we can see that for any arbitrary <math id="A2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, if</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">is not zero then:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A^{\left(s\right)}_{i,j}+A^{\left(L\right)}_{i,j}=\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{i,j}+0=\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{i,j}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A^{\left(s\right)}_{i,j}+A^{\left(L\right)}_{i,j}=\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{i,j}+0=\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{i,j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">On the other hand, if <math id="A2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero then we have:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A^{\left(s\right)}_{i,j}+A^{\left(L\right)}_{i,j}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A^{\left(s\right)}_{i,j}+A^{\left(L\right)}_{i,j}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~0+\left(Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~0+\left(Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{i,j}-\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{i,j}-\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{ij}+0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{ij}+0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{ij}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{ij},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">where in the second equality, we use <math id="A2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}=\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\right)\left(K^{\left(L\right)}+K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}=\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\right)\left(K^{\left(L\right)}+K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, we show the second condition of the support basis that <math id="A2.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(s\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(s\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint matrices.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows directly from Definition <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem4" title="Definition B.4. ‣ B.2 Support Basis ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>: if <math id="A2.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}+Q^{\left(L\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A2.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}_{i,j}=\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}_{i,j}=\left(QK^{\top}\right)_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(s\right)}_{i,j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(s\right)}_{i,j}=0</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise <math id="A2.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(L\right)}_{i,j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(L\right)}_{i,j}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\left(s\right)}_{i,j}=\left(Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\left(s\right)}_{i,j}=\left(Q^{\left(s\right)}\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}\right)_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we provide a concrete example of the support basis to better illustrate this decomposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example B.7</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(The support basis <math id="A2.Thmtheorem7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{A^{\left(L\right)},A^{\left(s\right)}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{A^{\left(L\right)},A^{\left(s\right)}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A2.Thmtheorem7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=\begin{bmatrix}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\\
e&amp;f\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>b</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>c</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>e</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>f</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=\begin{bmatrix}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\\
e&amp;f\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\top}=\begin{bmatrix}g&amp;i&amp;k\\
h&amp;j&amp;l\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{2\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>g</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>i</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>k</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>h</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>j</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>l</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\top}=\begin{bmatrix}g&amp;i&amp;k\\
h&amp;j&amp;l\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{2\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math>. With a given threshold <math id="A2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,k&gt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,k&gt;T</annotation></semantics></math>, and all other entries of <math id="A2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> are smaller than <math id="A2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, by Definition <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition B.3. ‣ B.2 Support Basis ‣ Appendix B (BATCH) GAUSSIAN KERNEL DENSITY ESTIMATION VIA SINGLE THRESHOLD SUPPORT BASIS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>, we have</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;0\\
c&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}}_{Q^{\left(L\right)}}+\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}a&amp;b\\
0&amp;d\\
e&amp;f\end{bmatrix}}_{Q^{\left(s\right)}}=\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\\
e&amp;f\end{bmatrix}}_{Q}~~\text{and}~~\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;0&amp;k\\
0&amp;0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}}_{\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}}+\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}g&amp;i&amp;0\\
h&amp;j&amp;l\end{bmatrix}}_{\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}}=\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}g&amp;i&amp;k\\
h&amp;j&amp;l\end{bmatrix}}_{K^{\top}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>c</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>b</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>e</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>f</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup></munder></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>b</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>c</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>e</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>f</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mi>Q</mi></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mi>k</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></munder></mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>g</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>i</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>h</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>j</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>l</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></munder></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>g</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>i</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>k</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>h</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>j</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>l</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;0\\
c&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}}_{Q^{\left(L\right)}}+\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}a&amp;b\\
0&amp;d\\
e&amp;f\end{bmatrix}}_{Q^{\left(s\right)}}=\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\\
e&amp;f\end{bmatrix}}_{Q}~~\text{and}~~\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;0&amp;k\\
0&amp;0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}}_{\left(K^{\left(L\right)}\right)^{\top}}+\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}g&amp;i&amp;0\\
h&amp;j&amp;l\end{bmatrix}}_{\left(K^{\left(s\right)}\right)^{\top}}=\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}g&amp;i&amp;k\\
h&amp;j&amp;l\end{bmatrix}}_{K^{\top}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This setup allows us to decompose the matrix product <math id="A2.Thmtheorem7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> into a sum of disjoint components:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\begin{bmatrix}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\\
e&amp;f\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}g&amp;i&amp;k\\
h&amp;j&amp;l\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>b</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>c</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>e</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>f</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>g</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>i</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>k</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>h</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>j</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>l</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\begin{bmatrix}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\\
e&amp;f\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}g&amp;i&amp;k\\
h&amp;j&amp;l\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\begin{bmatrix}ag+bh&amp;ai+bj&amp;ak+bl\\
cg+dh&amp;ci+dj&amp;ck+dl\\
eg+hf&amp;ei+fj&amp;ek+fl\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~\begin{bmatrix}ag+bh&amp;ai+bj&amp;ak+bl\\
cg+dh&amp;ci+dj&amp;ck+dl\\
eg+hf&amp;ei+fj&amp;ek+fl\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;0&amp;ak+bl\\
cg+dh&amp;ci+dj&amp;ck+dl\\
0&amp;0&amp;ek+fl\end{bmatrix}}_{A^{\left(L\right)}}+\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}ag+bh&amp;ai+bj&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;0\\
