<div id="A11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further evaluate the performance of LLaDA-8B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> on a range of standard benchmarks, including GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, MMLU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, Hellaswag <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite>, ARC-easy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, and ARC-challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.
In our method, we compute the attention score matrices exactly for the largest 10% and smallest 10% of entries, while approximating the remaining 80% with Chebyshev polynomials.
In contrast, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> applies Chebyshev polynomial approximation uniformly across all entries of the attention score matrices.
Table <a href="#A11.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Performance on Downstream Tasks ‣ Appendix K EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports the results. Our method demonstrates a clear advantage over <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> in practical settings. With a degree-4 Chebyshev approximation, our approach incurs less than a 3% drop in accuracy compared to exact attention. With a degree-6 Chebyshev approximation, the performance is nearly indistinguishable from the original computation. By contrast, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> fails to produce meaningful results, yielding zero accuracy across all benchmarks.
The superiority of our method over <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> stems from two key factors.
First, in multi-layer transformer architectures, approximation errors in the attention computation accumulate across blocks; our hybrid strategy is substantially more robust to such error propagation.
Second, large entries in <math id="A11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A11.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> are particularly sensitive to approximation error. By computing these entries exactly, our method avoids the large errors that <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> cannot, since it treats all entries uniformly.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A12" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix L </span>MORE RELATED WORK</h2>

<section id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Attention regression problems</h5>

<div id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Another line of work that attempts to reduce the computational complexity of attention approximation transforms the attention computation into regression problems and applies the approximate Newton method to solve them. These methods use sketching matrices to reduce the dimensionality of the Hessian in the Newton method, thereby accelerating the algorithm with provable guarantees. However, most of these works simplify the attention computation problem (Definition <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 1.2 (The exact attention computation). ‣ 1.1 Problem Formulation ‣ 1 INTRODUCTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> proposes a softmax regression formulation,</p>
<table id="A12.EGx231" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A12.Ex452"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A12.Ex452.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\left\|\langle\exp\left(Ax\right),{\bf 1}_{n}\rangle^{-1}\exp\left(Ax\right)-c\right\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\left\|\langle\exp\left(Ax\right),{\bf 1}_{n}\rangle^{-1}\exp\left(Ax\right)-c\right\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the value matrix is completely ignored. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> studies a rescaled version of softmax regression,</p>
<table id="A12.EGx232" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A12.Ex453"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A12.Ex453.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\left\|\exp\left(Ax\right)-\langle\exp\left(Ax\right),{\bf 1}_{n}\rangle c\right\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\left\|\exp\left(Ax\right)-\langle\exp\left(Ax\right),{\bf 1}_{n}\rangle c\right\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> analyzes an exponential regression problem,</p>
<table id="A12.EGx233" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A12.Ex454"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A12.Ex454.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}}\left\|\exp\left(AA^{\top}\right)x-b\right\|_{2}^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}}\left\|\exp\left(AA^{\top}\right)x-b\right\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where both <math id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> are omitted. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> is the only work that does not make any such simplifications, studying the attention regression problem</p>
<table id="A12.EGx234" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A12.Ex455"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A12.Ex455.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{X,Y\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}}\left\|D\left(X\right)^{-1}\exp\left(A_{1}XA_{2}^{\top}\right)A_{3}Y-B\right\|_{F}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{X,Y\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}}\left\|D\left(X\right)^{-1}\exp\left(A_{1}XA_{2}^{\top}\right)A_{3}Y-B\right\|_{F}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">However, the approximate Newton method can only solve convex problems, whereas attention computation is inherently non-convex. To apply the approximate Newton method, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> still relies on regularization terms and the choice of a good initialization point for the iterative procedure—assumptions that limit the applicability of the proposed algorithm.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sketching</h5>

<div id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a large data matrix <math id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, earlier works have centered on designing a sketching matrix <math id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\ll n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\ll n</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for every <math id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|SAx\|_{2}^{2}=(1\pm\epsilon)\|Ax\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>±</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|SAx\|_{2}^{2}=(1\pm\epsilon)\|Ax\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the approximation error. A matrix <math id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> with this property is known as a subspace embedding for <math id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. This idea has become a standard tool across many machine learning tasks, including linear regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>, (weighted) low-rank approximation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>, tensor power method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, online weighted matching problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, low-rank matrix completion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, <math id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-means clustering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, and attention mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Large language models</h5>

<div id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A12.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Besides the line of works that focus on the computational efficiency of LLMs, such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, there is another line of work on memory efficiency, such as LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, GaLore <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite>, I3S <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>, KV-Cache compression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, and CoVE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite>. Another line of work focuses on analyzing the softmax unit in attention, such as binary hypothesis testing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, softmax regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>. Other works focus on the circuit complexity of LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and limitations of LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. In reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite> design reinforcement learning targeted attack on LLMs, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> propose the reinforcement learning framework of LLM agents. Furthermore, the application of large transformers with long-context capabilities is not limited to language processing; broader domains such as time-series prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>, <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> are also of practical importance. Extending and validating our method to preserve the accuracy of large transformer models on these tasks is, therefore, a worthwhile direction for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A13" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix M </span>QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION</h2>

<div id="A13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we empirically validate our assumption that the entries of query and key matrices resemble sub-Gaussian distributions on transformer architectures such as TinyLlama-1.1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>, LLaDA-8B-Base <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, OPT-1.3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite>, and Phi-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> across multiple layers. Their model IDs are GSAI-ML/LLaDA-8B-Base (LLaDA-8B-Base), facebook/opt-1.3b (OPT-1.3B), TinyLlama/TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0 (TinyLlama-1.1B), and microsoft/phi-2 (Phi-2).</p>
</div>
<div id="A13.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A13.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To empirically validate our assumption, we visualize the distribution of the entries of <math id="A13.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A13.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> across multiple layers, generated by natural human-like tokens (Appendix <a href="#A13.SS2" title="M.2 TinyLlama-1.1B ‣ Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M.2</span></a>, <a href="#A13.SS3" title="M.3 OPT-1.3B ‣ Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M.3</span></a>, <a href="#A13.SS4" title="M.4 LLaDA-8B-Base ‣ Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M.4</span></a>, and <a href="#A13.SS5" title="M.5 Phi-2 ‣ Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M.5</span></a>). We observe that for all of the layers of attention in these transformer architectures (TinyLlama-1.1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>, LLaDA-8B-Base <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, OPT-1.3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite>, and Phi-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>), the entry distributions are sub-Gaussian-like, which satisfies the “practical assumption” made in this paper.
In Appendix <a href="#A13.SS1" title="M.1 TinyLlama-1.1B With Repeated Token ‣ Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M.1</span></a> we present the entry distribution of <math id="A13.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A13.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of TinyLlama-1.1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite> by deliberately constructing a non-human-like token sequence consisting of the character “h” repeated 2048 times. For these tokens, the distributions of the entries of <math id="A13.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A13.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> are more dispersed in the early layers (layers 0–2, Figures <a href="#A13.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ M.1 TinyLlama-1.1B With Repeated Token ‣ Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>–<a href="#A13.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ M.1 TinyLlama-1.1B With Repeated Token ‣ Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). However, starting from layer 3 onward, all entries of <math id="A13.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A13.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> become increasingly concentrated (Figures <a href="#A13.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ M.1 TinyLlama-1.1B With Repeated Token ‣ Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>–<a href="#A13.F26" title="Figure 26 ‣ M.1 TinyLlama-1.1B With Repeated Token ‣ Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>). LLMs are trained on natural human-like tokens, so it is expected that the entries of <math id="A13.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A13.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> remain tightly concentrated across all layers. Our result in Appendix <a href="#A13.SS1" title="M.1 TinyLlama-1.1B With Repeated Token ‣ Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M.1</span></a> can be viewed as a worst-case scenario: even if the entries are not well centered around the mean in the first few iterations, they become more tightly concentrated as <math id="A13.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> increases.</p>
</div>
<div id="A13.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A13.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Appendix <a href="#A13.SS2" title="M.2 TinyLlama-1.1B ‣ Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M.2</span></a>, we show that in TinyLlama-1.1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>, after the first few layers, the entries of <math id="A13.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A13.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> become tightly concentrated with light tails. We present the entry distribution of <math id="A13.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A13.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of TinyLlama-1.1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite> from layer 0 to layer 21. In Appendix <a href="#A13.SS3" title="M.3 OPT-1.3B ‣ Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M.3</span></a>, we present the entry distribution of <math id="A13.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A13.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of OPT-1.3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite> from layer 0 to layer 23.
In Appendix <a href="#A13.SS4" title="M.4 LLaDA-8B-Base ‣ Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M.4</span></a>, we present the entry distribution of <math id="A13.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A13.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of LLaDA-8B-Base <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> from layer 0 to layer 31. In Appendix <a href="#A13.SS5" title="M.5 Phi-2 ‣ Appendix M QUERY AND KEY ENTRIES DISTRIBUTION ‣ Support Basis: Fast Attention Beyond Bounded Entries" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M.5</span></a>, we present the entry distribution of <math id="A13.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A13.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> of Phi-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> from layer 0 to layer 31.</p>
</div>
<section id="A13.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">M.1 </span>TinyLlama-1.1B With Repeated Token</h3>

<div id="A13.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A13.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the entry distribution of <math id="A13.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A13.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in TinyLlama-1.1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite> from layer 0 to layer 21. The token sequence consists of the character “h” repeated 2048 times.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A13.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01643v1/QK_0.svg" id="A13.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/340;" width="476" height="340"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Distribution of entries in the query and key matrices of Layer 0 in TinyLlama-1.1B.
The red dashed lines mark the thresholds <math id="A13.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm\sqrt{\log n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm\sqrt{\log n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A13.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01643v1/QK_1.svg" id="A13.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/340;" width="476" height="340"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Distribution of entries in the query and key matrices of Layer 1 in TinyLlama-1.1B.
The red dashed lines mark the thresholds <math id="A13.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm\sqrt{\log n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm\sqrt{\log n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A13.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01643v1/QK_2.svg" id="A13.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/340;" width="476" height="340"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Distribution of entries in the query and key matrices of Layer 2 in TinyLlama-1.1B.
The red dashed lines mark the thresholds <math id="A13.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm\sqrt{\log n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm\sqrt{\log n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A13.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01643v1/QK_3.svg" id="A13.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/340;" width="476" height="340"></object>
